WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:06.450
So this is lecture 10.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:09.770
We're going to take a brief
respite from scaling laws.

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:12.800
And we're going to
talk about inference.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:16.530
So the question of inference
is a very simple one.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.680
Given a fixed model
that we've trained,

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:20.610
generate responses
given prompts.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.350
First, we're going to
start by understanding

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:30.060
what the implications
of inference are.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:33.410
And the workload
that it entails.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:35.090
And then we're going
to talk about ways

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:37.470
of making inference faster.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:39.830
And throughout this
lecture, you're

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:41.960
going to see that there's
a lot of-- inference

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:44.180
is a very deep topic.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:47.042
Actually, we didn't do
inference last year in lecture.

00:00:47.042 --> 00:00:48.750
So this is the first
year we're doing it.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:50.385
But there's actually
many, many topics

00:00:50.385 --> 00:00:52.010
that could span
multiple lectures which

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:54.680
I'll try to condense into one.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:58.470
So inference shows up in
multiple different places.

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:01.530
The most obvious place is if you
actually want to use a model,

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:03.380
you want to use it to chat.

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:06.930
You're using cursor or
something to do code completion.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:09.410
If you're running batch
data processing job

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:12.890
using your language model,
all of these cases demand

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:15.020
inference because you
need to generate tokens

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:16.800
from your actual model.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.960
But it also shows up
in other contexts.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.830
If you want to even
evaluate your model,

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:26.060
let's say on instruction
following need to do inference.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:29.330
There is a lot of interest
in test-time compute, which

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:33.020
means thinking more
before you actually

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:37.740
output the final answer.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:40.190
And that's also more
inference because thinking

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:42.000
is basically generate tokens.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.460
And then, finally,
even training itself.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.280
If you're using
reinforcement learning,

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:50.270
you need to sample
responses and then

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:52.050
evaluate them based
on some reward

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:54.540
and that also
requires inference.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:58.330
So inference isn't just I
want to put up a chatbot demo.

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:00.400
Inference actually
is going to underlie

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:03.740
many of the basic functions
of a language model.

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:05.210
And even though
it's one lecture,

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:09.400
I want to stress how important
it is for many things.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:10.900
And we'll probably
come back to this

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:16.330
when we talk about an
alignment later in the class.

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:18.560
So now inference is important.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:21.500
So the theme of this
class is efficiency.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:23.960
And efficiency clearly matters.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:26.080
Training is a one-time
cost, but inference

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:27.440
you repeat multiple times.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:34.610
So here's some anecdotal stats
on why inference is a big deal.

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:35.680
So

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:39.200
Sam says OpenAI generates
100 billion words a day,

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:42.010
which is quite a lot.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:45.320
And even Cursor, which is
not that new of a product,

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:48.280
is allegedly generating
a billion lines

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:51.110
of accepted code each day.

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:56.790
So it's just giving you an
idea of how much inference

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:00.600
is accounting for and
it costs of inference

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:05.250
compared to training are
definitely increasing.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:10.060
So how do you measure what
good inference looks like/

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:13.740
So there's
Time-To-First-Token, TTFT.

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:16.470
So this is how long
an individual user

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:19.630
needs to wait before any
generation happens at all.

00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:22.098
And this matters clearly for
interactive applications.

00:03:22.098 --> 00:03:23.640
If you have a big
prompt and then you

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:25.330
have to wait there
for 10 seconds,

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:27.790
that may not be a
good user experience.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:30.750
Latency is how fast
tokens are arriving

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:35.070
after maybe the first token.

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:38.640
This also matters for
interactive applications.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:41.350
Throughput is something
a bit different.

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:43.860
Throughput is how
many tokens in general

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:47.350
are generated per not
for overall users.

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:50.160
So this is particularly
useful in batch processing

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:51.250
applications.

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:53.400
So you can think about
the throughput is

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:56.650
high throughput doesn't
mean low latency,

00:03:56.650 --> 00:04:00.630
because some requests might
just take a very long time

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:02.140
and you still have
high throughput.

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:07.740
Latency is like the worst
case of an individual user.

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:10.470
So what do you need
to think about when

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:13.440
you think about the
efficiency of inference?

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:18.420
So in training, the
key idea is that you

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:21.670
get to see all the tokens, at
least a supervised training,

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:24.610
which means that you can
parallelize over the sequence.

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:27.203
This is exploited heavily
in the transformer.

00:04:27.203 --> 00:04:28.870
So you've done the
transformer training.

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:31.350
You know that you basically
construct these tensors

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:32.590
over the entire sequence.

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:36.370
And it's just like
tensor matmuls

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:38.820
and then you get your output.

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:41.737
But the key defining
feature of inference,

00:04:41.737 --> 00:04:43.320
at least for
transformers, is that you

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:45.340
have to generate sequentially.

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:50.400
You can't parallelize because
the generation of a token

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:52.750
depends on all of the past.

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:55.730
So this is going to be
the key thing that's going

00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:57.990
to make inference a lot harder.

00:04:57.990 --> 00:04:59.870
And in particular,
it's going to be harder

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:02.550
to utilize all the
compute that's available.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:04.050
And it's going to
be memory-limited,

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:06.620
as we'll see in detail later.

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:09.140
So a lot of people
are doing inference.

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:11.960
Anyone who's actually has
a product and platform

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:15.440
quickly realizes that these
costs in doing large models

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:16.680
is going to go up.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:20.930
So they spend a lot of
time and engineering effort

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:22.620
trying to reduce that time.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:26.030
So both providers serving
close models and providers

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:32.750
serving open weight models pay
a lot of attention to inference.

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:36.153
More so than I think the average
academic, because we're not

00:05:36.153 --> 00:05:37.320
actually serving any models.

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:38.510
We're just training
and getting a score

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:39.750
and putting in the paper.

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:41.660
But people who are
actually serving models

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:44.780
pay a lot of attention
to inference.

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:47.210
So there's also a bunch
of open source packages

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:52.810
which are interesting
to look at as well.

00:05:52.810 --> 00:06:00.650
So I want to understand the
inference workload in detail.

00:06:00.650 --> 00:06:04.870
So I'm going to review
briefly this transformer

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:08.002
math that you did
in assignment one

00:06:08.002 --> 00:06:09.460
and we talked a
little bit about it

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:12.170
during the first week of class.

00:06:12.170 --> 00:06:17.420
So this is from the
scaling jax-ml book,

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:21.230
which is something you guys
should really take a look at.

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:23.170
I think it does an
excellent job of outlining

00:06:23.170 --> 00:06:26.920
many of the key concepts here.

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:29.260
And they have this
really nice diagram

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:34.270
that shows essentially the
computation graph taken in input

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:38.710
and having it go through the
attention and the MLP layers.

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:41.830
In particular, we're going
to use this notation so just

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:44.030
to review this quickly.

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:47.380
So B is the number of
sequences in your batch.

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:48.760
L is the number of layers.

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:51.350
T is the sequence length.

00:06:51.350 --> 00:06:53.380
You can think about it
as the number of tokens

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:57.430
you're going to
generate or query using.

00:06:57.430 --> 00:06:59.680
S is also the sequence
length, but how many

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:02.630
you're kind of conditioning
on in your prompt.

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:03.830
V is the vocabulary.

00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:05.500
D is the dimensionality
of the model.

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:11.020
F is the MLP hidden dimension,
which is usually four times D.

00:07:11.020 --> 00:07:13.910
H is the attention
head dimension.

00:07:13.910 --> 00:07:16.070
N is the number of query heads.

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:19.600
So generally N times
H equals D, and then

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:22.930
in GQA, Group Query
Attention, you

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:25.820
have a different number key
value heads as query heads.

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:33.220
Usually K is smaller than N
And G is the number of groups.

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:36.280
So K times G equals N.

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:38.510
And this diagram shows
that you take your X.

00:07:38.510 --> 00:07:44.650
You feed through
the QKV matrices

00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:47.835
and you do a bunch of things.

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:56.520
So remember that the FLOPs
required for a feedforward pass

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:59.460
is 6 times the number
of tokens, which

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:02.830
is B times T times the
number of parameters.

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:06.300
Plus for the tension
there's another order of T.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:11.770
So T times T is T
squared dependence.

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:15.580
So let's also review
arithmetic intensity,

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:17.555
which is going to
help us characterize

00:08:17.555 --> 00:08:18.930
when something is
compute-limited

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:20.730
versus memory-limited.

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:25.090
So just to start with
the basic matmul.

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:30.480
So let's take a matrix X
which is B by D and a matrix

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:32.110
W, D by F.

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:37.919
And just to give some
color to this computation,

00:08:37.919 --> 00:08:40.150
B is the batch size, D
is the hidden dimension,

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:44.580
and F is the projection
matrix in the MLP.

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:50.540
So now let's do count the
number of FLOPs and memory read

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:54.630
and writes for just
doing X times W.

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:58.020
So we're going to start
with initialize to 0.

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:01.520
And what one has to
do for this is we're

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:06.660
going to read X from HBM.

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:10.400
So that means it incurs
a memory cost of 2 times

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:14.570
B times D assuming
everything is in bf16.

00:09:14.570 --> 00:09:19.910
You also read W. So
that's 2 times D times F.

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:26.900
Then you do the matmul and that
incurs 2 times B times D times F

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:27.570
FLOPs.

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:29.490
So remember, this is
from the first lecture.

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:31.790
So hopefully this is review.

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:33.650
And then you have to
write it back out which

00:09:33.650 --> 00:09:37.310
is you have to pay
another transfer.

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:42.390
So the total number of
FLOPs is just the matmul.

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:43.940
And the number of
bytes transferred

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:47.070
is essentially the size
of all the matrices

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:49.060
that are read and written.

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:53.280
And arithmetic intensity
is basically the ratio.

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:56.190
So the ratio is this expression.

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:59.610
And in general, just to
simplify things a bit,

00:09:59.610 --> 00:10:06.270
generally the batch size is much
less than D and F. B may be 100

00:10:06.270 --> 00:10:11.070
and a D and F might be
thousands or tens of thousands.

00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:16.080
So I'm using SymPy here
just to keep myself

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:18.540
from making silly mistakes.

00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:23.430
So basically, I'm letting
C go to infinity and D

00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:27.250
scales as C times B and
F scales as C times B.

00:10:27.250 --> 00:10:31.290
And that gets you a
simplified equation of B.

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:36.840
So the arithmetic intensity is
B for this particular matrix

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:37.766
multiplication.

00:10:43.810 --> 00:10:48.550
So the way to interpret
this is how many

00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:52.780
FLOPs are done per byte
that was transferred?

00:10:52.780 --> 00:10:58.180
So now the second part is you
look at the accelerator, which

00:10:58.180 --> 00:11:06.040
for H100 flops per second is
989 teraflops, memory bandwidth

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:09.640
3.3 bytes per second.

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:12.130
And you divide,
and that gives you

00:11:12.130 --> 00:11:14.900
what is called the
accelerator intensity.

00:11:14.900 --> 00:11:20.410
And if you look at the
computation intensity, which

00:11:20.410 --> 00:11:23.197
is B, If it's greater than
accelerator intensity,

00:11:23.197 --> 00:11:24.530
that means your compute-limited.

00:11:24.530 --> 00:11:27.560
That means you're able to
use all the GPUs or TPUs.

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:31.070
And if you're less than that,
then your memory-limited,

00:11:31.070 --> 00:11:33.470
which is bad.

00:11:33.470 --> 00:11:36.950
And so your compute-limited in
this matrix multiplication case,

00:11:36.950 --> 00:11:41.950
if B is greater
than 295 for a H100.

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:44.210
And all this is a bit idealized.

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:46.580
The actual details.

00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:50.980
This is giving you a
first-order approximation.

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:53.740
So in an extreme
case, so generally

00:11:53.740 --> 00:11:57.230
if you that means if you use
batches of size let's say 300,

00:11:57.230 --> 00:11:59.800
then you'll be going to be
able to saturate the GPU.

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:02.120
But what happens if your
batch is really small?

00:12:02.120 --> 00:12:05.200
So in particular B equals 1,
which essentially corresponds

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:07.250
to a matrix vector product.

00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:10.240
Then the arithmetic
intensity is basically 1.

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:12.680
And that is really, really bad.

00:12:12.680 --> 00:12:18.670
That means you're going to
be memory-limited which kind

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:22.780
of makes sense because basically
you're reading and writing this

00:12:22.780 --> 00:12:23.710
D times--

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:26.648
or actually you're just
reading this D times F matrix

00:12:26.648 --> 00:12:28.690
and you're performing
essentially the same number

00:12:28.690 --> 00:12:29.930
of FLOPs.

00:12:29.930 --> 00:12:32.080
So the ratio between
the FLOPs and the reads

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:35.150
is the same, which gives
you 1, and 1 is bad.

00:12:35.150 --> 00:12:37.900
You want a lot of FLOPs to
be done for any memory read

00:12:37.900 --> 00:12:41.730
because memory reads are slow.

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:45.570
But this is in essence, what
happens with generation.

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:47.440
Because you're proceeding
token by token,

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:51.480
we'll see that basically
your arithmetic intensity

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:53.370
is going to be 1.

00:12:53.370 --> 00:12:57.450
And that's why generation is
going to be memory-limited

00:12:57.450 --> 00:12:59.040
and not compute-limited.

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:01.050
So this is a very
simple example that I

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:05.320
think gets at the core of why
generation is going to be slow.

00:13:05.320 --> 00:13:08.160
So maybe I'll pause
and take any questions

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:13.110
on this just to make
sure everyone's clear.

00:13:13.110 --> 00:13:15.240
Yeah.

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:16.890
When doing inference,
if you have

00:13:16.890 --> 00:13:20.062
a batch size of larger than 1?

00:13:20.062 --> 00:13:22.020
I think I heard the
question, why don't we have

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:23.500
a batch size of more than 1?

00:13:23.500 --> 00:13:25.540
So I'll get to why you can.

00:13:25.540 --> 00:13:28.410
But there's batch size is
going to mean batch size

00:13:28.410 --> 00:13:30.620
times sequence length later.

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:42.420
So in summary, matrix
multiplications

00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:44.350
are the core computation.

00:13:44.350 --> 00:13:47.100
So we just studied a
matrix multiplication

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:51.390
and counted the number of FLOPs
it requires over the number read

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:52.240
and writes.

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:56.430
And we show that ratio, which
is the accelerator intensity

00:13:56.430 --> 00:13:59.040
depends on one of the
dimensions in case

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:00.880
in this case, the
batch dimension.

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:03.930
And that's why big matrices
are good because that

00:14:03.930 --> 00:14:06.010
can saturate your compute.

00:14:06.010 --> 00:14:10.210
Whereas if you have even
a thin matrix, B equals 1,

00:14:10.210 --> 00:14:14.550
that's really bad because you're
spending a lot of time reading

00:14:14.550 --> 00:14:19.500
from memory and not
doing that much compute.

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:23.970
So now let's talk
about the arithmetic

00:14:23.970 --> 00:14:28.270
intensity of inference.

00:14:28.270 --> 00:14:32.460
So let's just get
more into the weeds

00:14:32.460 --> 00:14:34.000
of what inference looks like.

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:37.740
So the naive thing
you can imagine doing

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:41.870
and all these nice pictures
are taken from this book

00:14:41.870 --> 00:14:43.830
is that you have a transformer.

00:14:43.830 --> 00:14:47.190
You give a prompt in.

00:14:47.190 --> 00:14:52.530
It gives you logits over the
vocabulary of the next token.

00:14:52.530 --> 00:14:54.210
And you just sample from that.

00:14:54.210 --> 00:14:58.470
And then once you get that
attach it to the prompt.

00:14:58.470 --> 00:15:00.570
And then you feed it
through the transformer,

00:15:00.570 --> 00:15:04.850
and you look at the logit
sample again and you repeat.

00:15:04.850 --> 00:15:10.610
So that's the most
naive thing to do.

00:15:10.610 --> 00:15:14.840
And the complexity
here is pretty bad

00:15:14.840 --> 00:15:22.070
because each token you generate
is like N squared or T squared,

00:15:22.070 --> 00:15:27.020
computation through
the transformer.

00:15:27.020 --> 00:15:29.330
So that's no good.

00:15:29.330 --> 00:15:32.570
But if you look at
this closely, you'll

00:15:32.570 --> 00:15:36.420
notice that you're doing
a lot of redundant work.

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:43.990
All the work in basically
encoding the prefix

00:15:43.990 --> 00:15:46.420
basically stays the same.

00:15:46.420 --> 00:15:52.070
So this for a bidirectional
transformer would be different.

00:15:52.070 --> 00:15:55.550
But at least for autoregressive
causal transformer,

00:15:55.550 --> 00:15:58.210
it is the case
that you should be

00:15:58.210 --> 00:16:00.530
able to share a lot
between prefixes.

00:16:00.530 --> 00:16:05.710
And so the solution is you cache
and you cache in the high band

00:16:05.710 --> 00:16:08.680
HBM because that's where
you have enough space

00:16:08.680 --> 00:16:09.620
to store stuff.

00:16:09.620 --> 00:16:15.220
So this is what it looks if you
have a KV cache schematically.

00:16:15.220 --> 00:16:18.650
So you take your prompt.

00:16:18.650 --> 00:16:21.760
The prefill step is you feed
it through the transformer

00:16:21.760 --> 00:16:25.660
and you compute this
KV cache, and then you

00:16:25.660 --> 00:16:28.730
generate the logits
over the next token.

00:16:28.730 --> 00:16:33.280
And then you put that into--

00:16:33.280 --> 00:16:37.998
take that generate a
token and the cache,

00:16:37.998 --> 00:16:40.040
and then you can feed it
through the transformer.

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:41.410
But you've already
computed these,

00:16:41.410 --> 00:16:42.868
so you don't have
to do that again.

00:16:42.868 --> 00:16:47.510
You just need to compute this
new KV vector for this token.

00:16:47.510 --> 00:16:52.450
And now that allows you to more
quickly generate the next token,

00:16:52.450 --> 00:16:53.950
and so on.

00:16:53.950 --> 00:16:56.440
So basically, you're
filling up this KV cache

00:16:56.440 --> 00:17:00.130
which corresponds to the
tokens that you've either

00:17:00.130 --> 00:17:03.400
pre-filled with or that
you've generated so far.

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:05.829
So instead of T
squared per token,

00:17:05.829 --> 00:17:12.190
it's going to be more like
T. So concretely the KV cache

00:17:12.190 --> 00:17:14.480
is for every sequence
in your batch,

00:17:14.480 --> 00:17:16.795
for every token
in your sequence,

00:17:16.795 --> 00:17:18.920
for every layer of the
transformer, for every head,

00:17:18.920 --> 00:17:22.270
you're going to store
a H-dimensional vector.

00:17:22.270 --> 00:17:25.119
So you might think that this is
going to take a lot of memory

00:17:25.119 --> 00:17:27.700
and you wouldn't be wrong.

00:17:27.700 --> 00:17:30.290
So there's two
stages of inference.

00:17:30.290 --> 00:17:33.700
So prefill is you're given your
prompt encoded in a vector.

00:17:33.700 --> 00:17:35.807
So this is just like
what you do in training.

00:17:35.807 --> 00:17:36.640
It's parallelizable.

00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:37.140
It's fast.

00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:39.520
You're compute-limited
and life is good.

00:17:39.520 --> 00:17:41.560
And then you're
doing generation,

00:17:41.560 --> 00:17:43.680
which is you're generating
response tokens one

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:45.095
by one sequentially.

00:17:45.095 --> 00:17:46.470
And this is the
part that's going

00:17:46.470 --> 00:17:51.360
to give us a lot of trouble
in terms of efficiency.

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:54.090
So now let's compute
the FLOPs and memory

00:17:54.090 --> 00:17:56.902
I/O both for the transformer.

00:17:56.902 --> 00:17:58.860
So we're going to break
it down into MLP layers

00:17:58.860 --> 00:18:01.380
and the attention layers.

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:05.610
And just for
notationwise, we're going

00:18:05.610 --> 00:18:08.550
to do this computation with
S being the number of tokens

00:18:08.550 --> 00:18:09.808
we're conditioning on.

00:18:09.808 --> 00:18:11.350
Think about the
length of the prompt.

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:13.590
And T is the number
of tokens we're

00:18:13.590 --> 00:18:17.910
generating or querying using.

00:18:17.910 --> 00:18:21.300
And in prefilled,
T is going to be S

00:18:21.300 --> 00:18:25.390
because we're not
generating T tokens,

00:18:25.390 --> 00:18:29.500
but we're querying using
each of these tokens.

00:18:29.500 --> 00:18:32.395
And a generation
where T is just 1.

00:18:34.970 --> 00:18:37.070
So hopefully the
matrix multiplication

00:18:37.070 --> 00:18:38.660
is so fresh in your
head because this

00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:41.780
is going to be essentially
that but a little bit more

00:18:41.780 --> 00:18:43.800
complicated because
it's a transformer.

00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:47.270
So we're going to count the
FLOPs and bytes generated.

00:18:47.270 --> 00:18:56.900
So first, we're going to take
X which is a T by D matrix.

00:18:56.900 --> 00:18:59.060
I think maybe these
T should be S's.

00:18:59.060 --> 00:19:08.300
But anyway, that involves
doing a bunch of transfers,

00:19:08.300 --> 00:19:13.070
Basically the size of that
matrix times 2 because bf16.

00:19:13.070 --> 00:19:16.880
Then there's the three-way
matrices-- the up projection,

00:19:16.880 --> 00:19:18.450
the gate, and the
down projection.

00:19:18.450 --> 00:19:23.990
They're all the same num
size up to transposition.

00:19:23.990 --> 00:19:26.360
So you need to transfer those.

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:33.020
Then you do the up projection.

00:19:33.020 --> 00:19:35.130
That's some number of flops.

00:19:35.130 --> 00:19:37.680
So B times D times T times F.

00:19:37.680 --> 00:19:41.090
So whenever you multiply
two tensors basically

00:19:41.090 --> 00:19:42.560
the contracting
dimension only gets

00:19:42.560 --> 00:19:45.710
counted once whereas the
other dimensions you just kind

00:19:45.710 --> 00:19:47.780
gather together.

00:19:47.780 --> 00:19:49.350
You need to write it out.

00:19:49.350 --> 00:19:53.790
You also have the gate
which is this same thing.

00:19:53.790 --> 00:19:54.960
You write it out.

00:19:54.960 --> 00:20:00.150
You compute your non-linearity.

00:20:00.150 --> 00:20:02.660
You multiply some stuff
and you down project.

00:20:02.660 --> 00:20:06.260
And that's a B times
T times D times

00:20:06.260 --> 00:20:09.390
F, which is basically
the same number of FLOPs,

00:20:09.390 --> 00:20:14.730
and you write out the result.

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:18.140
So if you look at
the counting, I

00:20:18.140 --> 00:20:23.763
guess maybe you can
check the results.

00:20:23.763 --> 00:20:26.180
Actually, you don't need to
check it because this is SymPy

00:20:26.180 --> 00:20:28.300
and it's guaranteed
to be correct.

00:20:31.222 --> 00:20:34.630
But again, we're going to
assume that B times D is

00:20:34.630 --> 00:20:39.490
much smaller than D and F. And
we get that the intensity is

00:20:39.490 --> 00:20:41.450
B times T.

00:20:41.450 --> 00:20:45.340
So this is analogous to the
matrix multiplication case

00:20:45.340 --> 00:20:48.850
where the arithmetic intensity,
which we want to be high

00:20:48.850 --> 00:20:50.950
depends on how
large your batch is

00:20:50.950 --> 00:20:54.760
and how many tokens you're
essentially generating.

00:20:54.760 --> 00:20:58.300
So now if you look at
the two stages, pre-fill,

00:20:58.300 --> 00:21:00.880
life is good remember,
because we can just

00:21:00.880 --> 00:21:02.330
make BT large enough.

00:21:02.330 --> 00:21:03.710
You use a batch size.

00:21:03.710 --> 00:21:07.210
Even a batch size of
1 actually is maybe OK

00:21:07.210 --> 00:21:10.030
if you have long
enough sequence.

00:21:10.030 --> 00:21:11.960
So that's not a problem.

00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:15.490
Now generation, this
is where it becomes

00:21:15.490 --> 00:21:17.418
a little bit harder
because you're

00:21:17.418 --> 00:21:18.710
generating one token at a time.

00:21:18.710 --> 00:21:21.530
So T is 1.

00:21:21.530 --> 00:21:24.940
So if T is 1, that
means for BT to be

00:21:24.940 --> 00:21:27.140
large you need B to be large.

00:21:27.140 --> 00:21:31.060
And B is essentially the
number of concurrent requests.

00:21:31.060 --> 00:21:34.270
So this is of interesting
because you're

00:21:34.270 --> 00:21:37.777
efficiency depends on
having large batch sizes.

00:21:37.777 --> 00:21:39.610
Because I mean,
intuitively, it makes sense.

00:21:39.610 --> 00:21:42.380
If you can take a lot of
requests, batch them together,

00:21:42.380 --> 00:21:49.810
then you can get better
efficiency, at least throughput.

00:21:49.810 --> 00:21:53.510
But this also
depends on what B is.

00:21:53.510 --> 00:21:57.470
Because if you're only getting
a few requests at a time,

00:21:57.470 --> 00:22:00.160
then you're not going to be
able to use your hardware very

00:22:00.160 --> 00:22:02.530
efficiently.

00:22:02.530 --> 00:22:06.340
And this speaks to the
very dynamic aspect

00:22:06.340 --> 00:22:10.360
of inference, which we'll come
back to later in the lecture.

00:22:10.360 --> 00:22:13.030
So now what about attention?

00:22:13.030 --> 00:22:16.570
It turns out attention
is even worse for reasons

00:22:16.570 --> 00:22:18.260
I'll try to get into.

00:22:18.260 --> 00:22:23.050
So let's do the counting
FLOPs bytes transferred.

00:22:23.050 --> 00:22:32.350
So I'm going to read the
QKV matrices from HBM.

00:22:32.350 --> 00:22:36.850
I'm going to compute
the attention, which

00:22:36.850 --> 00:22:42.220
is a matrix, which is Q times
K and the number of FLOPs

00:22:42.220 --> 00:22:46.120
is B times S times T times
D. So remember S and T are

00:22:46.120 --> 00:22:47.420
the same during prefilled.

00:22:47.420 --> 00:22:52.510
So that's your sequence length
squared times B times D.

00:22:52.510 --> 00:22:57.850
And then I'm only looking at
the matrix multiplications

00:22:57.850 --> 00:23:01.360
because the FLOPs from the
other steps don't really matter.

00:23:01.360 --> 00:23:03.760
And then you project out to--

00:23:03.760 --> 00:23:07.520
sorry, you take a
combination of this and V.

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:11.230
So actually, this is
mathematically incorrect

00:23:11.230 --> 00:23:12.890
because there's some
softmaxes there.

00:23:12.890 --> 00:23:16.910
But the essence of the
math models are the same.

00:23:16.910 --> 00:23:19.220
So that's the same
number of FLOPS.

00:23:19.220 --> 00:23:23.420
And then you write to HBM.

00:23:23.420 --> 00:23:27.110
And here I'm
assuming there would

00:23:27.110 --> 00:23:29.900
be more bytes transferred if
you didn't use flash attention.

00:23:29.900 --> 00:23:31.400
Flash attention
means that you don't

00:23:31.400 --> 00:23:36.482
have to keep on writing back
to HBM into intermediate steps.

00:23:36.482 --> 00:23:41.220
But the order is actually
not really affected.

00:23:41.220 --> 00:23:43.280
So qualitatively,
it doesn't really

00:23:43.280 --> 00:23:45.600
matter whether you use
flash attention or not.

00:23:45.600 --> 00:23:49.520
But the math here depends on
the-- the constants matter.

00:23:49.520 --> 00:23:53.210
But let's look at the flops
and the bytes transferred.

00:23:53.210 --> 00:23:58.160
And if you divide
and simplify, you

00:23:58.160 --> 00:24:00.810
get this rather nice expression.

00:24:00.810 --> 00:24:03.860
I mean nice in that it's
simple, not nice in that it's

00:24:03.860 --> 00:24:09.950
good efficiency, which is S
times T divided by S plus T.

00:24:09.950 --> 00:24:14.040
So let's try to
interpret this a bit.

00:24:14.040 --> 00:24:20.270
So in prefill, T equals S.
So that means you're prefll

00:24:20.270 --> 00:24:24.680
intensity is order S. So
that's good because as long

00:24:24.680 --> 00:24:27.990
as you have long
enough sequences,

00:24:27.990 --> 00:24:32.480
then you're good to go.

00:24:32.480 --> 00:24:36.770
And generally, the sequences you
assume are kind of long enough.

00:24:36.770 --> 00:24:39.800
During generation,
however, you'll

00:24:39.800 --> 00:24:44.330
see that the density
is essentially 1.

00:24:44.330 --> 00:24:45.600
S over S plus 1.

00:24:45.600 --> 00:24:47.990
But that's basically one.

00:24:47.990 --> 00:24:50.350
And remember one is really bad.

00:24:54.510 --> 00:24:59.030
But notice like what there's
no dependence on B at all.

00:24:59.030 --> 00:25:04.760
So unlike in MLPs,
remember in MLPs,

00:25:04.760 --> 00:25:09.260
the generation the prefill
was BT, which is great.

00:25:09.260 --> 00:25:10.850
And then arithmetic
intensity was

00:25:10.850 --> 00:25:14.300
B, which was not great
because it depends

00:25:14.300 --> 00:25:17.030
on the whims of your
users and workloads

00:25:17.030 --> 00:25:19.800
but still could
be larger than 1.

00:25:19.800 --> 00:25:23.260
Whereas for attention,
it's actually

00:25:23.260 --> 00:25:25.030
just always less
than 1, no matter

00:25:25.030 --> 00:25:29.650
how long your sequences are
how many users there are,

00:25:29.650 --> 00:25:30.860
it's always 1.

00:25:30.860 --> 00:25:36.490
So why is this intuitively that
there's no dependence on B,

00:25:36.490 --> 00:25:38.500
the batch dimension?

00:25:38.500 --> 00:25:43.360
So the reason is that in
the MLP layers, intuitively,

00:25:43.360 --> 00:25:48.910
every sequence hits
the same MLP weights

00:25:48.910 --> 00:25:54.160
whereas in the attention layer
each sequence has its own KV

00:25:54.160 --> 00:25:55.630
cache.

00:25:55.630 --> 00:25:59.060
Because the KV cache
is sequence-specific,

00:25:59.060 --> 00:26:05.320
which means that you
can't really, use--

00:26:05.320 --> 00:26:07.210
In MLP case, you can
read all the weights

00:26:07.210 --> 00:26:09.740
and then you process
a batch intuitively,

00:26:09.740 --> 00:26:13.660
whereas in an attention case
every sequence kind of requires

00:26:13.660 --> 00:26:14.420
additional memory.

00:26:14.420 --> 00:26:19.710
You don't get any kind of
savings if you batch them up.

00:26:19.710 --> 00:26:23.370
Mathematically, I guess you
can look at it through here

00:26:23.370 --> 00:26:26.620
where the number of
FLOPs, there's a B here,

00:26:26.620 --> 00:26:29.760
which is expected, but the
number of bytes transferred

00:26:29.760 --> 00:26:34.350
is B times-- there's
a scaling and B,

00:26:34.350 --> 00:26:39.660
so when you divide
that B cancels.

00:26:39.660 --> 00:26:44.530
Whereas over here,
there is a B here.

00:26:44.530 --> 00:26:47.680
But we're assuming
that DF dominates.

00:26:47.680 --> 00:26:50.640
So when you divide,
basically, there's

00:26:50.640 --> 00:26:53.740
no B essentially left
in the denominator.

00:26:53.740 --> 00:26:55.590
So you can look at
it mathematically

00:26:55.590 --> 00:26:58.050
or you can just kind
of reason about it

00:26:58.050 --> 00:27:03.250
intuitively as
for the attention,

00:27:03.250 --> 00:27:10.230
the KV cache is every sequence
its own unique snowflake.

00:27:10.230 --> 00:27:14.310
So the summary is prefill
is compute-limited

00:27:14.310 --> 00:27:17.580
whereas generation
is memory-limited.

00:27:17.580 --> 00:27:22.810
The MLP arithmetic intensity is
B, which to make good enough,

00:27:22.810 --> 00:27:24.940
you need a bunch of
concurrent requests.

00:27:24.940 --> 00:27:28.650
But attention intensity is
1, and it's also impossible

00:27:28.650 --> 00:27:30.300
improve that.

00:27:30.300 --> 00:27:36.420
OK, I'll pause a bit
for any questions.

00:27:36.420 --> 00:27:41.040
So let's move on.

00:27:41.040 --> 00:27:45.660
So now we know that inference,
thanks to generation,

00:27:45.660 --> 00:27:47.650
is memory-limited.

00:27:47.650 --> 00:27:51.900
Let's try to study the
throughput and latency

00:27:51.900 --> 00:27:55.860
at least in theory.

00:27:55.860 --> 00:28:03.160
So let's focus on let's see.

00:28:03.160 --> 00:28:06.760
So we're going to
make some assumptions.

00:28:06.760 --> 00:28:10.840
So all of this is napkin math
is a little bit stylized,

00:28:10.840 --> 00:28:12.990
but it gives you
roughly the right kind

00:28:12.990 --> 00:28:15.760
of scaling and the right
way to think about things.

00:28:15.760 --> 00:28:18.560
So we're going to assume that
communication and compute can

00:28:18.560 --> 00:28:24.530
be perfectly overlapped, which
is obviously false but it's

00:28:24.530 --> 00:28:31.130
good enough for making
these qualitative estimates.

00:28:31.130 --> 00:28:34.490
So what we're going to do is
we're going to instantiate

00:28:34.490 --> 00:28:39.720
the latency and throughput
for a Llama 2_13b on H100.

00:28:39.720 --> 00:28:46.040
So for a 13b, here
are the values.

00:28:46.040 --> 00:28:48.420
Let's just put the sequence
length to be 1,000,

00:28:48.420 --> 00:28:51.570
hidden dimension to be
model dimension to be 5,000.

00:28:51.570 --> 00:28:55.320
4 times-- Actually, I don't
know if that's not 4 times.

00:28:55.320 --> 00:28:59.450
But anyway, F is some multiple
of that number of heads, number

00:28:59.450 --> 00:29:06.540
key value, number of query
heads number of key value heads,

00:29:06.540 --> 00:29:08.100
which for Llama 2 is the same.

00:29:08.100 --> 00:29:11.370
We'll get to that
point later, and so on.

00:29:11.370 --> 00:29:14.600
And for the memory bandwidth
of H100, that's the number.

00:29:14.600 --> 00:29:17.970
So that's the config.

00:29:17.970 --> 00:29:23.190
And we're going to compute the
memory latency and throughput.

00:29:30.110 --> 00:29:34.620
So first, let's just quickly
get the number of parameters.

00:29:34.620 --> 00:29:37.310
You guys did this in assignment
one, so I won't belabor this

00:29:37.310 --> 00:29:41.330
but it's some
expression that depends

00:29:41.330 --> 00:29:48.930
on all the different variables,
and to store the parameters.

00:29:48.930 --> 00:29:54.200
We're going to use bf16 because
inference is generally going

00:29:54.200 --> 00:29:56.990
to be 16-bit not 32-bit.

00:29:56.990 --> 00:29:58.350
So we're going to multiply by 2.

00:29:58.350 --> 00:30:03.240
So that's the memory
that the parameters take.

00:30:03.240 --> 00:30:04.530
We don't need gradients.

00:30:04.530 --> 00:30:07.320
We don't need optimizer states
because we're not training.

00:30:07.320 --> 00:30:11.330
But we do have to store
the KV cache, which

00:30:11.330 --> 00:30:14.770
are the sum of the activations.

00:30:14.770 --> 00:30:16.270
Not all the
activations, but some

00:30:16.270 --> 00:30:20.740
of them for every sequence
of length S. And how much

00:30:20.740 --> 00:30:23.800
do we have to per store
per sequence it's basically

00:30:23.800 --> 00:30:31.150
the sequence length times
the number key value

00:30:31.150 --> 00:30:33.550
heads times the dimension of
that head times the number

00:30:33.550 --> 00:30:38.470
of layers times basically 2
for basically both the key

00:30:38.470 --> 00:30:41.800
and the value, and 2 for bf16.

00:30:41.800 --> 00:30:46.540
So that's how much
the cache size takes.

00:30:46.540 --> 00:30:49.300
And so the total memory
is the batch size

00:30:49.300 --> 00:30:53.470
times the cache per
sequence, plus the memories,

00:30:53.470 --> 00:30:55.540
plus the parameter size.

00:30:55.540 --> 00:31:00.820
So now latency is going
to be determined by memory

00:31:00.820 --> 00:31:02.600
I/O. Remember it's
memory-limited,

00:31:02.600 --> 00:31:06.220
so we're just going to
compute how much memory it

00:31:06.220 --> 00:31:11.510
needs to be transferred into
the GPU to do this computation.

00:31:11.510 --> 00:31:14.860
And simply memory over
the memory bandwidth.

00:31:14.860 --> 00:31:18.980
And throughput is essentially
the inverse of latency,

00:31:18.980 --> 00:31:22.600
but scaled up by B because
we're looking at generating

00:31:22.600 --> 00:31:26.180
B tokens in parallel.

00:31:26.180 --> 00:31:32.560
So now if we substitute
our Llama to config,

00:31:32.560 --> 00:31:35.150
we'll see that the number
of parameters checks out.

00:31:35.150 --> 00:31:38.650
It's 13 billion roughly.

00:31:38.650 --> 00:31:41.890
The memory latency
and throughput

00:31:41.890 --> 00:31:44.320
have these expressions.

00:31:44.320 --> 00:31:46.750
So memory grows obviously.

00:31:46.750 --> 00:31:48.380
This is the parameter size.

00:31:48.380 --> 00:31:52.900
This is the key value
cache size times B.

00:31:52.900 --> 00:31:57.910
Latency also goes up as a
function of B. Throughput

00:31:57.910 --> 00:32:01.220
increases, but you'll see that
it increases up to a point.

00:32:01.220 --> 00:32:04.420
The B shows up in both the
numerator and the denominator.

00:32:04.420 --> 00:32:06.760
So there's limits to how much
you can stretch throughput

00:32:06.760 --> 00:32:09.400
even if you could fit
everything in memory.

00:32:09.400 --> 00:32:16.650
So those are the expressions for
latency, throughput and memory

00:32:16.650 --> 00:32:19.260
for this particular model.

00:32:19.260 --> 00:32:22.210
So now let's instantiate
with different batch sizes.

00:32:22.210 --> 00:32:30.190
So if B equals 1, then the
latency is about 8 milliseconds.

00:32:30.190 --> 00:32:32.220
So every 8 milliseconds
you generate

00:32:32.220 --> 00:32:39.010
a token and the throughput
is 124 tokens per second OK.

00:32:39.010 --> 00:32:44.430
So that's 13b on a H100 if
you're using a batch size of 1.

00:32:44.430 --> 00:32:49.800
So now what happens if
you use batch size of 16?

00:32:49.800 --> 00:32:55.740
So you'll see that the
memory usage increases

00:32:55.740 --> 00:33:00.990
because you need to store the KV
cache for all 64 sequences now.

00:33:00.990 --> 00:33:06.030
The latency goes
up because you have

00:33:06.030 --> 00:33:08.020
to instead of just
processing one,

00:33:08.020 --> 00:33:11.300
you have to wait for
everything to finish.

00:33:11.300 --> 00:33:15.830
But the throughput also goes
up actually quite a lot.

00:33:15.830 --> 00:33:18.620
So you're seeing this
immediate trade-off

00:33:18.620 --> 00:33:21.710
between latency and throughput
if you want low latency

00:33:21.710 --> 00:33:26.460
you just use B equals 1.

00:33:26.460 --> 00:33:30.230
But if you want high throughput,
you want larger B in general.

00:33:30.230 --> 00:33:33.710
What happens if you use a batch
size of even larger, so 256?

00:33:36.680 --> 00:33:40.448
You'll see that the latency
goes up, the throughput goes up,

00:33:40.448 --> 00:33:41.990
but you see that
the throughput isn't

00:33:41.990 --> 00:33:44.240
going up that much because
you get diminishing returns

00:33:44.240 --> 00:33:45.680
after a while.

00:33:45.680 --> 00:33:50.760
But the most-- you can
actually do this on a H100

00:33:50.760 --> 00:33:55.650
because if you look at
the memory, it's 240 gigs,

00:33:55.650 --> 00:33:58.790
so it doesn't even fit.

00:33:58.790 --> 00:34:02.960
So batch size you can only
increase to a certain point

00:34:02.960 --> 00:34:05.000
because of memory.

00:34:05.000 --> 00:34:07.160
So just to recap,
there's a trade off

00:34:07.160 --> 00:34:08.580
between latency and throughput.

00:34:08.580 --> 00:34:11.370
Smaller batch sizes,
yield better latency.

00:34:11.370 --> 00:34:16.580
Larger batch sizes
yield better throughput.

00:34:16.580 --> 00:34:20.330
And finally, last week, we
talked about parallelism

00:34:20.330 --> 00:34:21.120
for training.

00:34:21.120 --> 00:34:26.120
And it was kind of a
complicated, annoying.

00:34:26.120 --> 00:34:29.360
At least one type of
parallelism for inference

00:34:29.360 --> 00:34:32.900
is really, really
nice and simple.

00:34:32.900 --> 00:34:36.239
You just launch M
copies of the model.

00:34:36.239 --> 00:34:38.120
No communication
because the model

00:34:38.120 --> 00:34:39.690
you don't need to
update the models.

00:34:39.690 --> 00:34:43.730
The latency is the same and
the throughput increases by M.

00:34:43.730 --> 00:34:46.580
So that's pretty good.

00:34:46.580 --> 00:34:51.320
So always remember that
don't forget easy things.

00:34:51.320 --> 00:34:55.460
Now there are cases where if
you have a large enough model,

00:34:55.460 --> 00:34:59.670
then maybe it doesn't
even fit on a single GPU

00:34:59.670 --> 00:35:01.860
and you need to shard the model.

00:35:01.860 --> 00:35:04.970
And in this case, you
also want to start

00:35:04.970 --> 00:35:11.630
sharding the KV cache in some
cases to get better efficiency.

00:35:11.630 --> 00:35:14.385
For more details, check
out this book chapter.

00:35:17.330 --> 00:35:21.940
So the time-to-first-token,
which is a metric I mentioned

00:35:21.940 --> 00:35:26.570
earlier, is essentially a
function of the prefill.

00:35:26.570 --> 00:35:29.540
It's basically, how long does
it take to encode the prompt.

00:35:29.540 --> 00:35:33.080
And usually, this
is compute-limited,

00:35:33.080 --> 00:35:36.790
so you're basically
going as fast as you can.

00:35:36.790 --> 00:35:39.760
And there's not much you can
do about it given a fixed

00:35:39.760 --> 00:35:40.606
architecture.

00:35:46.030 --> 00:35:53.260
Sorry, you can improve it if
you reduce the batch size still.

00:35:53.260 --> 00:35:56.590
But if you want to
improve the throughput,

00:35:56.590 --> 00:35:59.440
you have to increase
the batch size.

00:36:02.480 --> 00:36:04.780
So any questions about that?

00:36:04.780 --> 00:36:08.770
So this was on computing
the throughput and latency.

00:36:08.770 --> 00:36:12.630
And because of the
memory limited argument

00:36:12.630 --> 00:36:18.870
that I gave in the previous
part, I just focus on memory

00:36:18.870 --> 00:36:21.580
and compute how many
bytes need to be sent.

00:36:21.580 --> 00:36:26.320
And that gives me a rough
bound on the latency.

00:36:26.320 --> 00:36:28.230
In practice, there
are some regimes

00:36:28.230 --> 00:36:31.770
where compute does matter,
but I'm ignoring that just

00:36:31.770 --> 00:36:35.250
to keep things simple.

00:36:35.250 --> 00:36:36.930
OK, question.

00:36:36.930 --> 00:36:40.110
Is this assuming a single GPU?

00:36:40.110 --> 00:36:41.765
Yes, this is assuming
a single GPU.

00:36:44.700 --> 00:36:48.000
While creating a batch
from multiple users,

00:36:48.000 --> 00:36:52.770
each of these prompts have to be
completed within the [INAUDIBLE]

00:36:52.770 --> 00:36:54.750
number of tokens generated.

00:36:54.750 --> 00:36:57.250
So there is empty
slots in these values.

00:36:57.250 --> 00:36:59.400
Yeah, so the question is
if you have multiple users

00:36:59.400 --> 00:37:00.550
and you're batching
them together,

00:37:00.550 --> 00:37:01.810
they might arrive
at different times.

00:37:01.810 --> 00:37:02.980
They're going to finish
at different times.

00:37:02.980 --> 00:37:04.410
So we're going to get to that.

00:37:04.410 --> 00:37:07.530
That's going to be a
special issue that we're

00:37:07.530 --> 00:37:09.750
going to have to deal with.

00:37:09.750 --> 00:37:11.080
Any other questions?

00:37:18.171 --> 00:37:21.870
So now we have a good handle
on what the inference workload

00:37:21.870 --> 00:37:23.220
looks like.

00:37:23.220 --> 00:37:25.630
We looked at the
arithmetic intensity.

00:37:25.630 --> 00:37:29.760
We looked at the transformer
inference with respect

00:37:29.760 --> 00:37:31.570
to arithmetic intensity.

00:37:31.570 --> 00:37:33.570
We saw that it was
memory-limited thanks

00:37:33.570 --> 00:37:36.450
to the attention where
the KV cache has to be

00:37:36.450 --> 00:37:38.170
special for every sequence.

00:37:38.170 --> 00:37:41.320
And then using that, we can
compute throughput and latency,

00:37:41.320 --> 00:37:45.360
which are the main inference
metrics that we care about.

00:37:45.360 --> 00:37:48.510
Now how do we make
things better?

00:37:48.510 --> 00:37:53.912
So there are some things that
you can do on that are lossless.

00:37:53.912 --> 00:37:55.120
You can write better kernels.

00:37:55.120 --> 00:37:57.580
You can improve your systems.

00:37:57.580 --> 00:38:01.430
But I would say that
the kind of there's

00:38:01.430 --> 00:38:06.680
a lot you can do if you're
willing to take shortcuts.

00:38:06.680 --> 00:38:09.830
And these are kind of really
interesting because technically

00:38:09.830 --> 00:38:13.220
this lecture is on
inference, but secretly it's

00:38:13.220 --> 00:38:15.980
on model architectures,
because what you'll see

00:38:15.980 --> 00:38:19.280
is that a lot of the changes
in model architecture

00:38:19.280 --> 00:38:22.530
are going to have direct
impact on inference,

00:38:22.530 --> 00:38:26.990
and we're actually inspired by
needing to do inference quickly.

00:38:26.990 --> 00:38:32.780
So the big bottleneck
here is the KV cache.

00:38:32.780 --> 00:38:35.120
because remember
memory-limited, which

00:38:35.120 --> 00:38:41.100
means that the less memory stuff
takes then the faster you go.

00:38:41.100 --> 00:38:45.630
Not just because of FLOPs
even though that's part of it.

00:38:45.630 --> 00:38:49.130
But mostly due to memory,
because it's mostly about memory

00:38:49.130 --> 00:38:50.040
transfers.

00:38:50.040 --> 00:38:52.380
If that's one thing you
take away from this lecture,

00:38:52.380 --> 00:38:57.380
it's like all about
the memory for speed.

00:38:57.380 --> 00:39:04.280
So the problem is that if you
just start walking away the KV

00:39:04.280 --> 00:39:06.450
cache, you might lose accuracy.

00:39:06.450 --> 00:39:10.040
So how can you make sure you
don't lose too much accuracy

00:39:10.040 --> 00:39:16.100
but still maintain
your KV cache small.

00:39:16.100 --> 00:39:20.930
So there's a bunch of ideas
I'm going to go through

00:39:20.930 --> 00:39:24.200
that all essentially try
to change the architecture

00:39:24.200 --> 00:39:25.560
to reduce your KV cache.

00:39:25.560 --> 00:39:28.820
Some of these ideas
I think you've seen,

00:39:28.820 --> 00:39:32.940
but I'll go through them kind
of in this more systematic way.

00:39:32.940 --> 00:39:36.510
So there's this idea called
grouped-query attention.

00:39:36.510 --> 00:39:40.200
So multi-headed attention, which
is the vanilla transformer,

00:39:40.200 --> 00:39:44.130
keeps around basically
a number of heads.

00:39:44.130 --> 00:39:47.480
And for each of that
number, you have same number

00:39:47.480 --> 00:39:51.080
of keys, values and queries.

00:39:51.080 --> 00:39:54.240
There was one time a
multi-query attention,

00:39:54.240 --> 00:39:58.790
which you only have
one key and one value.

00:39:58.790 --> 00:40:01.480
Basically, one key value had.

00:40:01.480 --> 00:40:04.130
Turned out that was
not very expressive.

00:40:04.130 --> 00:40:06.280
So there was a
intermediate point

00:40:06.280 --> 00:40:10.910
where you have a reduced
number of keys and values,

00:40:10.910 --> 00:40:13.870
and then you have more queries.

00:40:13.870 --> 00:40:15.770
So why are we doing this?

00:40:15.770 --> 00:40:18.860
Well, remember we want to
reduce the KB cache size.

00:40:18.860 --> 00:40:21.522
So the fewer keys and values
there are, the better.

00:40:21.522 --> 00:40:23.230
So the batch size and
the sequence length

00:40:23.230 --> 00:40:27.250
doesn't get changed, but
it's in the dimensionality

00:40:27.250 --> 00:40:30.370
of these vectors
don't change, but it's

00:40:30.370 --> 00:40:35.170
the number key value
heads that we're reducing.

00:40:35.170 --> 00:40:38.900
So that's basically the idea.

00:40:38.900 --> 00:40:44.980
And this paper shows that you
do get latency and throughput

00:40:44.980 --> 00:40:47.630
improvements, so
times per sample.

00:40:47.630 --> 00:40:54.760
And as you increase the number
of groups up to eight or so,

00:40:54.760 --> 00:40:58.450
basically there's a negligible--

00:40:58.450 --> 00:41:03.040
It's really fast compared
to the full attention.

00:41:03.040 --> 00:41:04.970
And as you increase
the number of groups,

00:41:04.970 --> 00:41:07.990
obviously you end
up at the original.

00:41:07.990 --> 00:41:12.220
So that's latency and
throughput improvements.

00:41:12.220 --> 00:41:17.350
And just to actually do
this more rigorously,

00:41:17.350 --> 00:41:20.660
we have our Llama 2_13b model.

00:41:20.660 --> 00:41:26.170
And if we compute
the statistics,

00:41:26.170 --> 00:41:30.560
this is using a
batch size of 64.

00:41:30.560 --> 00:41:34.210
Remember, this is what we got.

00:41:34.210 --> 00:41:38.200
I guess I should have printed
out latency here as well.

00:41:38.200 --> 00:41:45.520
And then if you run
it with GQA, you

00:41:45.520 --> 00:41:50.470
see that the memory is reduced
and the throughput goes way up.

00:41:50.470 --> 00:41:53.380
So this is actually great.

00:41:53.380 --> 00:41:57.360
So this is what happens
if I take the Llama 2_13b

00:41:57.360 --> 00:41:59.220
architecture and I just reduce.

00:42:02.740 --> 00:42:05.560
So for every key value head,
I have five query heads.

00:42:05.560 --> 00:42:07.485
That's 1-to-5 ratio means.

00:42:11.050 --> 00:42:13.230
This also means we can
use a larger batch size.

00:42:13.230 --> 00:42:16.300
Because remember last
time we tried to do 256,

00:42:16.300 --> 00:42:19.720
it didn't even fit
in an H100's memory.

00:42:19.720 --> 00:42:23.190
So now we can
actually comfortably

00:42:23.190 --> 00:42:24.840
fit into the H100
memory and then

00:42:24.840 --> 00:42:26.520
we can further
improve the throughput

00:42:26.520 --> 00:42:28.650
by using a larger batch size.

00:42:28.650 --> 00:42:31.900
So you can see a lot of
different effects here.

00:42:31.900 --> 00:42:35.710
By reducing the number
key value pairs,

00:42:35.710 --> 00:42:38.970
the memory of the
KV cache reduces.

00:42:38.970 --> 00:42:42.780
That means the throughput and
latency go up automatically

00:42:42.780 --> 00:42:44.770
because fewer memory transfers.

00:42:44.770 --> 00:42:46.510
And furthermore, as
a secondary effect,

00:42:46.510 --> 00:42:49.230
I can increase the batch
size within the GPU

00:42:49.230 --> 00:42:51.640
and that further
improves the throughput.

00:42:55.040 --> 00:42:58.370
So that's wonderful.

00:42:58.370 --> 00:43:00.990
We have to also make sure
the accuracy doesn't drop.

00:43:00.990 --> 00:43:05.540
So this is original
paper that shows

00:43:05.540 --> 00:43:08.610
that this is full attention.

00:43:08.610 --> 00:43:10.220
This is GQA.

00:43:10.220 --> 00:43:16.740
The time is much less, but the
accuracy is basically the same.

00:43:16.740 --> 00:43:21.230
Now what actually happened?

00:43:21.230 --> 00:43:25.080
So Llama 2 did not
use this ratio.

00:43:25.080 --> 00:43:31.220
But Llama 3 actually picked
up GQA and probably motivated

00:43:31.220 --> 00:43:33.140
by the kind of inference costs.

00:43:33.140 --> 00:43:35.150
Actually Llama 2,
I think, the 70--

00:43:35.150 --> 00:43:37.870
the large model did have gqa
but not the smaller ones.

00:43:40.920 --> 00:43:42.840
So that's a GQA.

00:43:42.840 --> 00:43:47.820
There is another way to
reduce the key value cache.

00:43:47.820 --> 00:43:50.000
And this comes from
DeepSeek So this is actually

00:43:50.000 --> 00:43:53.800
from the DeepSeek
V2 paper and it's

00:43:53.800 --> 00:43:56.170
called multi-head latent
tension, which Tatsu lectured

00:43:56.170 --> 00:43:59.830
about previously,
but I'll try to talk

00:43:59.830 --> 00:44:03.110
about it in the context of
inference and its implications.

00:44:03.110 --> 00:44:07.370
So the basic idea is
here's full attention.

00:44:07.370 --> 00:44:11.770
And GQA says, I'm going to
use fewer keys and values.

00:44:11.770 --> 00:44:16.280
MLA says I'm not going to change
the number key and values.

00:44:16.280 --> 00:44:20.290
I'm going to project these
into a lower dimensional space.

00:44:20.290 --> 00:44:23.420
So it's another way of
shrinking the KV size,

00:44:23.420 --> 00:44:27.100
but just, I guess, in
a different dimension.

00:44:27.100 --> 00:44:30.430
So instead of using
N times H dimensions

00:44:30.430 --> 00:44:37.300
for the KV cache
of each token, I'm

00:44:37.300 --> 00:44:40.360
going to project
down to C dimensions.

00:44:40.360 --> 00:44:42.230
And this is what DeepSeek did.

00:44:42.230 --> 00:44:46.360
It's actually quite aggressive
reduction from 16,000 to 512.

00:44:46.360 --> 00:44:49.130
Only wrinkle is that this
is not compatible with rope.

00:44:49.130 --> 00:44:52.360
So they need to add a few
more dimensions to input rope

00:44:52.360 --> 00:44:55.960
back in, but overall,
this is actually

00:44:55.960 --> 00:45:01.760
quite promising from a
KV reduction perspective.

00:45:01.760 --> 00:45:04.450
I'm not going to do the
math, but you can just

00:45:04.450 --> 00:45:07.330
trust me that you
can see how the KV

00:45:07.330 --> 00:45:08.900
cache would be reduced a lot.

00:45:08.900 --> 00:45:11.380
And you get to the same kind
of latency and throughput

00:45:11.380 --> 00:45:13.360
advantages.

00:45:13.360 --> 00:45:15.910
And in terms of
accuracy, they actually

00:45:15.910 --> 00:45:18.463
showed that compared to GQA--

00:45:24.550 --> 00:45:27.195
actually, maybe I'm showing
the wrong thing here.

00:45:30.220 --> 00:45:32.380
Image.

00:45:32.380 --> 00:45:37.243
I meant to show that the
MLA actually improves,

00:45:37.243 --> 00:45:38.660
but this table
does not show that.

00:45:38.660 --> 00:45:40.910
So I'll have to
dig that up later.

00:45:40.910 --> 00:45:47.920
But anyway it MLA does
preserve the accuracy as well.

00:45:47.920 --> 00:45:56.470
So there's another idea which
says, well, GQA basically

00:45:56.470 --> 00:46:01.180
you can think about it as a
sharing key value vectors,

00:46:01.180 --> 00:46:06.720
within a token and
within a sequence.

00:46:06.720 --> 00:46:09.000
But we can also
look at something

00:46:09.000 --> 00:46:12.730
called cross-layer attention,
which there's a paper on this,

00:46:12.730 --> 00:46:16.290
but I think many people have
been kind of thinking about this

00:46:16.290 --> 00:46:17.410
and doing this.

00:46:17.410 --> 00:46:19.930
So I don't know if this is
actually the first paper.

00:46:19.930 --> 00:46:23.370
But basically, if you look
at the transformer diagram,

00:46:23.370 --> 00:46:26.740
you have the key value
projection of one layer,

00:46:26.740 --> 00:46:31.410
and then you have the next layer
and these key value vectors

00:46:31.410 --> 00:46:35.100
are separate, usually.

00:46:35.100 --> 00:46:38.640
But the idea here with
CLA is that we're just

00:46:38.640 --> 00:46:41.710
going to use the same key
value projection across layers.

00:46:41.710 --> 00:46:46.410
That's why it's called
Cross Layer Attention.

00:46:46.410 --> 00:46:54.150
So just as GQA shares across
heads, CLA shares across layers.

00:46:54.150 --> 00:46:58.710
So here they show
that they empirically

00:46:58.710 --> 00:47:05.190
improve the Pareto frontier of
accuracy and the KV cache size.

00:47:05.190 --> 00:47:09.600
So KV cache size, which relates
to throughput and latency,

00:47:09.600 --> 00:47:11.320
you want it to be small.

00:47:11.320 --> 00:47:15.520
And you want perplexity
also to be small.

00:47:15.520 --> 00:47:19.730
So they're able to improve that.

00:47:22.950 --> 00:47:33.030
So notice that, for example, 64
heads, the cache size it gets

00:47:33.030 --> 00:47:34.800
reduced but the
validation perplexity

00:47:34.800 --> 00:47:36.810
does go up a little bit.

00:47:36.810 --> 00:47:40.800
But overall the
there's advantage

00:47:40.800 --> 00:47:46.310
in making that trade-off.

00:47:46.310 --> 00:47:50.240
OK, so there's yet
another way to do things.

00:47:50.240 --> 00:47:54.980
So local attention, which
has been explored actually

00:47:54.980 --> 00:47:58.770
quite a bit since even kind
of the there's a Longformer,

00:47:58.770 --> 00:48:01.340
there's an OpenAI
paper, and then Mistral

00:48:01.340 --> 00:48:04.940
and I think many others
use this as well.

00:48:04.940 --> 00:48:07.110
It's a very, I guess,
a natural idea.

00:48:07.110 --> 00:48:09.630
Instead of if you look at
a full attention diagram,

00:48:09.630 --> 00:48:14.090
it's dense N squared, and that's
where a lot of your complexity

00:48:14.090 --> 00:48:16.010
comes from.

00:48:16.010 --> 00:48:18.770
And basically,
the idea is you're

00:48:18.770 --> 00:48:26.720
going to just attend to only the
past K tokens, which means that

00:48:26.720 --> 00:48:29.577
in a KV cache, as you're
generating the sequence,

00:48:29.577 --> 00:48:31.160
you don't have to
remember everything.

00:48:31.160 --> 00:48:35.630
As soon as the token kind
of falls outside your window

00:48:35.630 --> 00:48:39.020
that you have attention,
you can just throw it away.

00:48:39.020 --> 00:48:44.710
So local attention is very
could say that the KV cache

00:48:44.710 --> 00:48:48.490
size remains constant as opposed
to growing with a sequence

00:48:48.490 --> 00:48:49.360
length.

00:48:49.360 --> 00:48:51.880
So this is really
good because that

00:48:51.880 --> 00:48:54.070
means that for
even long sequences

00:48:54.070 --> 00:48:57.345
you can have quite
a small cache.

00:49:02.080 --> 00:49:06.410
But the problem is that
this still hurts accuracy,

00:49:06.410 --> 00:49:09.340
because if you just think
about it like, why are we

00:49:09.340 --> 00:49:12.430
doing attention
instead of RNNs, is

00:49:12.430 --> 00:49:16.990
that we needed to have long
range model and long range

00:49:16.990 --> 00:49:17.900
dependencies.

00:49:17.900 --> 00:49:20.680
And this is in some
sense even to call it

00:49:20.680 --> 00:49:24.050
attention is a little bit
kind of overselling this.

00:49:24.050 --> 00:49:26.800
This is only looking
at the local context,

00:49:26.800 --> 00:49:29.350
which is not very expressive.

00:49:29.350 --> 00:49:33.940
So what you do here is you
can interleave local attention

00:49:33.940 --> 00:49:38.710
with full global
attention hybrid layers.

00:49:38.710 --> 00:49:43.810
So for example character I
used for every six layers

00:49:43.810 --> 00:49:47.500
they had one global attention
global layer and five

00:49:47.500 --> 00:49:48.650
local layers.

00:49:48.650 --> 00:49:50.290
So it looks
something in addition

00:49:50.290 --> 00:49:52.610
to cross layer attention.

00:49:52.610 --> 00:49:56.290
So it looks something
like this where

00:49:56.290 --> 00:50:02.230
full attention, every layer
you have to store the KV cache.

00:50:02.230 --> 00:50:08.170
And what they did is
that for every six layers

00:50:08.170 --> 00:50:11.590
you have the full attention,
but in between you

00:50:11.590 --> 00:50:13.340
have this local attention.

00:50:13.340 --> 00:50:19.060
And on top of that, they have
KV cache sharing locally,

00:50:19.060 --> 00:50:23.590
both for the local attention
and the global attention.

00:50:23.590 --> 00:50:26.380
So this is like all
the tricks kind of--

00:50:26.380 --> 00:50:32.200
not all the tricks, but many
of the tricks combine together.

00:50:32.200 --> 00:50:34.960
So in summary,
these are a few ways

00:50:34.960 --> 00:50:37.100
to reduce the KV cache size.

00:50:37.100 --> 00:50:40.510
Because remember inference
is memory-limited.

00:50:40.510 --> 00:50:42.160
So you want to reduce
the cache size,

00:50:42.160 --> 00:50:44.440
but you don't want to
hurt accuracy too much.

00:50:44.440 --> 00:50:45.940
And there are many
ways to do it.

00:50:45.940 --> 00:50:50.170
You can lower the
dimensionality of the KV cache.

00:50:50.170 --> 00:50:52.660
You can have few
KV cache vectors.

00:50:52.660 --> 00:50:57.340
You can reduce the
dimensionality of a keV vector.

00:50:57.340 --> 00:51:01.860
You can share the KV
cache across layers,

00:51:01.860 --> 00:51:07.980
and also you can use local
attention on some of the layers.

00:51:07.980 --> 00:51:11.580
Any questions about
this set of tricks

00:51:11.580 --> 00:51:13.100
for reducing the KV cache?

00:51:15.700 --> 00:51:16.200
Yeah.

00:51:16.200 --> 00:51:18.090
[INAUDIBLE] question
about the quality.

00:51:18.090 --> 00:51:22.620
And I feel like the
weights are all that you

00:51:22.620 --> 00:51:25.290
shared across the layers.

00:51:25.290 --> 00:51:29.490
Do you just have one set
of weights or just like KV

00:51:29.490 --> 00:51:34.005
And that's shared
across the [INAUDIBLE].

00:51:34.005 --> 00:51:37.252
The question is, are
the weights shared.

00:51:37.252 --> 00:51:39.460
So the KV cache is shared
but the weights are shared.

00:51:39.460 --> 00:51:45.120
So what happens is the weights
for doing the projection need

00:51:45.120 --> 00:51:45.700
to be shared.

00:51:45.700 --> 00:51:47.016
So there's some consistency.

00:51:50.560 --> 00:51:51.935
There was another question.

00:51:55.470 --> 00:51:59.190
The context size is
too large and then

00:51:59.190 --> 00:52:02.310
it increases the KV cache
as well context size

00:52:02.310 --> 00:52:05.670
when we prompt the [INAUDIBLE]
the context that is

00:52:05.670 --> 00:52:08.070
given to the translation model.

00:52:08.070 --> 00:52:11.380
it can become too long
and it increases the size.

00:52:11.380 --> 00:52:15.810
So we try reducing the size
summarizing the context.

00:52:15.810 --> 00:52:18.490
So the question is if you
have really long contexts,

00:52:18.490 --> 00:52:20.460
let's say your prompt
is huge, that's

00:52:20.460 --> 00:52:24.960
going to intrinsically
take a lot of KV cache.

00:52:24.960 --> 00:52:29.670
So all these tricks
can try to reduce that.

00:52:29.670 --> 00:52:31.470
You can do more
aggressive things

00:52:31.470 --> 00:52:35.340
that there's ideas like
gist tokens or ways

00:52:35.340 --> 00:52:38.900
to summarize the prompt, which
we're not going to talk about

00:52:38.900 --> 00:52:41.900
in this class, but there's ways
to have that address the long

00:52:41.900 --> 00:52:43.120
prompt situation as well.

00:52:47.942 --> 00:52:54.110
So now I'm going to talk about
even more radical ways of making

00:52:54.110 --> 00:52:57.900
inference go faster by
changing the transformer.

00:52:57.900 --> 00:53:00.260
So the KV cache,
these are basically

00:53:00.260 --> 00:53:03.410
variants of the transformer.

00:53:03.410 --> 00:53:10.400
But maybe you can actually
go outside the transformer

00:53:10.400 --> 00:53:13.790
and do better because
the transformer wasn't

00:53:13.790 --> 00:53:16.770
really designed with heavy
inference workloads in mind.

00:53:16.770 --> 00:53:20.310
They were just trying to train
a good model that efficiently.

00:53:20.310 --> 00:53:22.170
It was mostly about
training efficiency.

00:53:22.170 --> 00:53:25.980
And the autoregression,
as we pointed out,

00:53:25.980 --> 00:53:29.120
is really causing
this bottleneck here

00:53:29.120 --> 00:53:34.280
with the autoregression
plus the full attention.

00:53:34.280 --> 00:53:37.390
So we're going to talk
about two directions--

00:53:37.390 --> 00:53:39.640
state space models
and diffusion models.

00:53:39.640 --> 00:53:43.270
This is going to
be fairly quick.

00:53:43.270 --> 00:53:46.660
So the idea of
state space models

00:53:46.660 --> 00:53:50.800
is actually drawing ideas from
signal processing and control

00:53:50.800 --> 00:53:51.880
theory.

00:53:51.880 --> 00:53:54.040
Initially, the
motivation was trying

00:53:54.040 --> 00:53:58.660
to model long context sequences
without suffering the N squared

00:53:58.660 --> 00:53:59.420
blowup.

00:53:59.420 --> 00:54:01.610
So it wasn't necessarily
about inference speed.

00:54:01.610 --> 00:54:03.740
But it turns out if
you solve that problem,

00:54:03.740 --> 00:54:05.270
you get faster inference, too.

00:54:05.270 --> 00:54:09.370
So there's a kind of
early paper on S4, which

00:54:09.370 --> 00:54:13.990
uses classical state-space
models, which are basically

00:54:13.990 --> 00:54:17.620
these linear dynamical
systems, which

00:54:17.620 --> 00:54:21.790
are you've been used
to model long contexts

00:54:21.790 --> 00:54:26.800
and shoehorning them into the
kind of a modern neural setup.

00:54:26.800 --> 00:54:35.050
This work is nice in that it has
this RNN kind of interpretation

00:54:35.050 --> 00:54:41.650
due to the linearity structure,
and also has a convolution.

00:54:41.650 --> 00:54:43.340
Interpretation as well.

00:54:43.340 --> 00:54:49.510
So they published
this paper and show

00:54:49.510 --> 00:54:53.170
that I think worked really
well on these long context

00:54:53.170 --> 00:54:55.030
synthetic tasks.

00:54:55.030 --> 00:54:58.680
But what they found
is that, well, I

00:54:58.680 --> 00:55:03.790
guess what was discovered is
that they don't really work well

00:55:03.790 --> 00:55:06.850
for language modeling.

00:55:06.850 --> 00:55:08.990
And that's obviously
kind of a disappointment,

00:55:08.990 --> 00:55:11.170
because a lot of the
value of transformers

00:55:11.170 --> 00:55:13.840
is being able to
do language well.

00:55:13.840 --> 00:55:20.260
So in a series of papers,
they identified a set

00:55:20.260 --> 00:55:24.520
of synthetic tasks that captured
the essence of why these models

00:55:24.520 --> 00:55:26.150
weren't working well.

00:55:26.150 --> 00:55:28.550
And that's basically these
associative recall tasks.

00:55:28.550 --> 00:55:31.270
So here's a synthetic task
where you're given basically

00:55:31.270 --> 00:55:33.280
a sequence key value pairs.

00:55:35.860 --> 00:55:39.160
And the goal is to predict
basically lookup the key

00:55:39.160 --> 00:55:40.190
and output the value.

00:55:40.190 --> 00:55:43.940
So in some sense, it's kind
of a logically a trivial task.

00:55:43.940 --> 00:55:47.270
But it's long sequence because I
can have a lot key value pairs.

00:55:47.270 --> 00:55:50.930
And I'm going to have
to look far back.

00:55:50.930 --> 00:55:52.818
It can be arbitrarily
long dependence.

00:55:52.818 --> 00:55:54.610
And you can see that
local attention is not

00:55:54.610 --> 00:55:57.110
going to work very well, because
it's just going to remember

00:55:57.110 --> 00:55:58.640
the last few sequences.

00:55:58.640 --> 00:56:02.170
And well, the problem
with state-space models

00:56:02.170 --> 00:56:06.140
is that they're good for these
kind of signal processing tasks.

00:56:06.140 --> 00:56:10.440
But really, you need to go
isolate a particular key value

00:56:10.440 --> 00:56:10.940
pair.

00:56:10.940 --> 00:56:13.810
And pull out the
answer for those type

00:56:13.810 --> 00:56:15.740
of tasks it didn't really work.

00:56:15.740 --> 00:56:18.020
So there's a bunch of
work I'm not citing.

00:56:18.020 --> 00:56:24.160
There's like hyena, H3, and then
Mamba, which basically tweaked

00:56:24.160 --> 00:56:29.620
or change the H SSM to basically
handle these associative recall

00:56:29.620 --> 00:56:30.290
tasks.

00:56:30.290 --> 00:56:32.230
And eventually,
it worked better.

00:56:32.230 --> 00:56:36.750
Up to 1B scale was
matching transformers.

00:56:36.750 --> 00:56:43.590
And the idea of mamba has
been popular in scaled up even

00:56:43.590 --> 00:56:49.830
to a 52B MoE by 21 folks.

00:56:49.830 --> 00:56:54.610
Notice that in this case they
still had to use a transformer.

00:56:54.610 --> 00:56:58.932
So a transformer, but
only I guess eight layers.

00:56:58.932 --> 00:56:59.890
They had a transformer.

00:56:59.890 --> 00:57:02.580
The rest of them
were mamba layers.

00:57:02.580 --> 00:57:08.345
And so that still led to a
fairly big savings and speed up.

00:57:11.840 --> 00:57:15.180
But, more recently,
there's this kind

00:57:15.180 --> 00:57:19.290
of revival of this older
idea called linear attention,

00:57:19.290 --> 00:57:22.320
where instead of--

00:57:22.320 --> 00:57:25.920
let's see if I can
make this bigger.

00:57:25.920 --> 00:57:28.240
It's actually a
very simple idea.

00:57:28.240 --> 00:57:32.150
So you know local attention or
sliding window attention is.

00:57:32.150 --> 00:57:39.890
Linear attention is this
idea that you essentially--

00:57:39.890 --> 00:57:43.370
So basically in the
attention computation,

00:57:43.370 --> 00:57:48.170
there's a key and a
query and you dot product

00:57:48.170 --> 00:57:51.290
and you take the exp of that,
which is basically giving you

00:57:51.290 --> 00:57:53.010
a exp kernel.

00:57:53.010 --> 00:57:55.760
So you can basically take
a Taylor expansion of that

00:57:55.760 --> 00:57:58.700
and write that computation
as basically dot

00:57:58.700 --> 00:58:01.020
products of some nonlinear map.

00:58:01.020 --> 00:58:03.740
So then what you
essentially have is

00:58:03.740 --> 00:58:08.250
you can think about for
every key value position,

00:58:08.250 --> 00:58:11.400
you are basically applying
some of nonlinearity,

00:58:11.400 --> 00:58:13.670
blowing it up into some
space and then doing

00:58:13.670 --> 00:58:15.600
some linear computation over it.

00:58:15.600 --> 00:58:17.400
And because it's
linear attention,

00:58:17.400 --> 00:58:20.420
it actually kind of
behaves like an RNN

00:58:20.420 --> 00:58:25.890
and it's linear in the sequence
length rather than quadratic.

00:58:25.890 --> 00:58:27.780
I know that was a
little bit fast,

00:58:27.780 --> 00:58:31.970
but I just want to give
you the taste of it.

00:58:31.970 --> 00:58:35.650
And so this idea
has been actually

00:58:35.650 --> 00:58:37.520
scaled up quite successfully.

00:58:37.520 --> 00:58:41.140
So there's this
organization called

00:58:41.140 --> 00:58:46.090
MiniMax that's training
pretty legitimate models,

00:58:46.090 --> 00:58:50.980
up to 456 billion
parameter MoEs.

00:58:50.980 --> 00:58:54.350
And they use this basically
linear attention idea.

00:58:54.350 --> 00:58:59.920
Now they have to use full
attention still once in a while

00:58:59.920 --> 00:59:01.580
it seems.

00:59:01.580 --> 00:59:06.370
I don't think people have
been able to get around having

00:59:06.370 --> 00:59:09.640
some full attention,
but at least

00:59:09.640 --> 00:59:12.160
it seems like people
have been able to get

00:59:12.160 --> 00:59:15.370
rid of most of the full
attention in, most of the layers

00:59:15.370 --> 00:59:17.800
are not full attention
anymore, they're just

00:59:17.800 --> 00:59:20.350
either linear layers or
local attention layers which

00:59:20.350 --> 00:59:24.250
are much, much more efficient.

00:59:24.250 --> 00:59:29.890
OK, so the linear
plus local attention

00:59:29.890 --> 00:59:33.860
now are actually yielding
serious state of the art models.

00:59:33.860 --> 00:59:37.960
And it's probably safe
to say that, well, I

00:59:37.960 --> 00:59:41.900
don't know what exactly the
close model providers are doing,

00:59:41.900 --> 00:59:43.990
but I would suspect
that there will

00:59:43.990 --> 00:59:48.850
be at least as kind of advanced
in terms of as efficient as this

00:59:48.850 --> 00:59:50.160
and leveraging sparsity.

00:59:52.870 --> 00:59:55.030
So it's kind of an
interesting question when

00:59:55.030 --> 00:59:57.910
people ask like,
well, attention all

00:59:57.910 --> 00:59:59.710
you need and it's transformers?

00:59:59.710 --> 01:00:02.720
Well, yes and no.

01:00:02.720 --> 01:00:04.810
I mean, I guess in some
sense there is still

01:00:04.810 --> 01:00:06.615
the sense it's square there.

01:00:06.615 --> 01:00:08.240
Maybe it will be able
to get rid of it.

01:00:08.240 --> 01:00:10.120
But most of the
transformer has been

01:00:10.120 --> 01:00:15.250
like pretty radically changed by
having other much lighter weight

01:00:15.250 --> 01:00:16.010
components.

01:00:16.010 --> 01:00:18.040
And you're still
able to get much

01:00:18.040 --> 01:00:21.850
of the same kind of accuracies.

01:00:21.850 --> 01:00:26.070
And all of this is really
helpful for inference

01:00:26.070 --> 01:00:29.400
because on these non
full attention layers,

01:00:29.400 --> 01:00:32.700
you're basically
replacing the ordered T KV

01:00:32.700 --> 01:00:36.000
cache, which grows as a sequence
length with something that's

01:00:36.000 --> 01:00:38.010
constant.

01:00:38.010 --> 01:00:43.260
And there's papers
that follow up--

01:00:43.260 --> 01:00:46.060
I think they on the BASED paper.

01:00:46.060 --> 01:00:47.850
Where did that go?

01:00:47.850 --> 01:00:50.280
Either in this paper
or in follow up work

01:00:50.280 --> 01:00:54.480
analyzing basically the
trade off between the KV size

01:00:54.480 --> 01:00:59.400
and the ability to do various
types of recall tasks, which

01:00:59.400 --> 01:01:02.790
makes sense because if
you don't store very much

01:01:02.790 --> 01:01:04.660
won't be able to
solve certain tasks.

01:01:04.660 --> 01:01:06.300
But there is this
trade-off curve

01:01:06.300 --> 01:01:07.865
that you can try to play with.

01:01:10.840 --> 01:01:16.260
So that's all I'll say about
the state-space models.

01:01:16.260 --> 01:01:19.440
So now let's talk about a
completely different style

01:01:19.440 --> 01:01:21.480
of generation models.

01:01:21.480 --> 01:01:22.780
Diffusion models.

01:01:22.780 --> 01:01:24.590
So diffusion models
have been very popular

01:01:24.590 --> 01:01:26.173
in image generation,
but they turn out

01:01:26.173 --> 01:01:29.130
to be fairly tricky to
get working in text,

01:01:29.130 --> 01:01:32.880
although there recently have
been some advances here.

01:01:32.880 --> 01:01:36.080
So the idea of diffusion
is that instead

01:01:36.080 --> 01:01:38.930
of generating autoregressively,
you just generate every token

01:01:38.930 --> 01:01:39.870
in parallel.

01:01:39.870 --> 01:01:43.888
So obviously you only do
that, via some simple layer.

01:01:43.888 --> 01:01:45.180
It's not going to be very good.

01:01:45.180 --> 01:01:47.013
You can't generate all
the words in parallel

01:01:47.013 --> 01:01:48.630
and expect it to be coherent.

01:01:48.630 --> 01:01:50.840
But what you do is
you iterate and you

01:01:50.840 --> 01:01:54.890
keep on refining this
generation until it

01:01:54.890 --> 01:01:59.120
gets to your final
generation that you output.

01:01:59.120 --> 01:02:04.980
And the idea behind
generating in parallel,

01:02:04.980 --> 01:02:08.700
you're no longer
autoregressively bound,

01:02:08.700 --> 01:02:11.840
and that generating all
tokens in parallel well

01:02:11.840 --> 01:02:13.920
can be done in parallel.

01:02:13.920 --> 01:02:18.260
So you get to saturate
your GPUs relatively easy

01:02:18.260 --> 01:02:21.290
as long as your context
length is large enough.

01:02:21.290 --> 01:02:25.340
So recently, there's
this Inception Labs

01:02:25.340 --> 01:02:29.960
has produced some pretty
interesting models.

01:02:29.960 --> 01:02:31.500
There's not much
written about them,

01:02:31.500 --> 01:02:35.280
but you can see a demo of
the generation and process.

01:02:35.280 --> 01:02:39.720
It's just kind of generates
code instantaneously,

01:02:39.720 --> 01:02:41.580
but it's obviously
kind of broken code.

01:02:41.580 --> 01:02:46.910
And then it's kind
of refines over time.

01:02:46.910 --> 01:02:51.500
And this is one of
their benchmarks

01:02:51.500 --> 01:02:53.760
that show that, at
least on coding,

01:02:53.760 --> 01:02:57.050
I'm not sure about other
tasks, that if you look

01:02:57.050 --> 01:03:00.530
at the tokens per second,
these models are way out here

01:03:00.530 --> 01:03:03.770
in terms of speed compared to
anything that's transformer.

01:03:03.770 --> 01:03:10.130
Even Jamba, remember, it was
like a hybrid Mamba transformer

01:03:10.130 --> 01:03:14.240
architecture is quite slow
compared to these diffusion

01:03:14.240 --> 01:03:15.180
models.

01:03:15.180 --> 01:03:19.558
So now whether diffusion models
will be kind of general purpose

01:03:19.558 --> 01:03:22.100
and powerful enough in all of
these, that remains to be seen.

01:03:22.100 --> 01:03:27.520
But I think you have such
a lead on the token speed

01:03:27.520 --> 01:03:33.190
here that even if you I think
you can put more compute

01:03:33.190 --> 01:03:40.330
and recover some of the
accuracy losses if you need to.

01:03:40.330 --> 01:03:43.510
So the summary here
is that I think

01:03:43.510 --> 01:03:46.960
this whole kind of
novel architecture thing

01:03:46.960 --> 01:03:51.820
is actually really
exciting for inference

01:03:51.820 --> 01:03:55.840
because they allow
you to sidestep

01:03:55.840 --> 01:03:57.410
kind of fundamental obstacles.

01:03:57.410 --> 01:04:00.230
So if you're dealing
with attention,

01:04:00.230 --> 01:04:03.010
you just have this
fundamental KV cache obstacle

01:04:03.010 --> 01:04:07.760
that you can quantize, you can
optimize, but it's still there.

01:04:07.760 --> 01:04:11.000
And so by making a kind
of state-space model,

01:04:11.000 --> 01:04:13.400
you're shrinking that
to a constant size.

01:04:13.400 --> 01:04:16.510
And as long as you can keep up
the accuracy, which is big if,

01:04:16.510 --> 01:04:18.590
then you win big time.

01:04:18.590 --> 01:04:20.180
Same with the diffusion models.

01:04:20.180 --> 01:04:22.520
Autoregressive generation
is a key bottleneck.

01:04:22.520 --> 01:04:25.850
Now if you just generalize it,
generate things in parallel.

01:04:25.850 --> 01:04:28.970
Now all of a sudden, you
change the game completely.

01:04:28.970 --> 01:04:31.870
So there's much more
work to be done here

01:04:31.870 --> 01:04:34.840
in improving inference.

01:04:34.840 --> 01:04:38.950
So as you can see now,
inference is the inference game

01:04:38.950 --> 01:04:42.200
is much broader than it
seems at first sight.

01:04:42.200 --> 01:04:43.930
It's not really
about necessarily

01:04:43.930 --> 01:04:46.243
the system's optimizations
to make it fast,

01:04:46.243 --> 01:04:47.660
although you
obviously need those.

01:04:47.660 --> 01:04:52.330
But I think the real gains are
coming from real radical changes

01:04:52.330 --> 01:04:54.580
in architecture.

01:04:54.580 --> 01:04:56.420
OK, so I have about
10 minutes left.

01:04:56.420 --> 01:04:58.870
I'll go through these quickly.

01:04:58.870 --> 01:05:00.560
Quantization and model pruning.

01:05:00.560 --> 01:05:03.887
So quantization the
key idea is just reduce

01:05:03.887 --> 01:05:05.095
the precision of the numbers.

01:05:07.900 --> 01:05:09.920
Very easy to do.

01:05:09.920 --> 01:05:12.520
And the thought is
that less memory

01:05:12.520 --> 01:05:16.460
means a less bytes
transferred higher latency--

01:05:16.460 --> 01:05:20.610
Sorry, this should be lower
latency, higher throughput.

01:05:20.610 --> 01:05:23.140
And you do have to
worry about accuracy.

01:05:23.140 --> 01:05:25.770
Of course, that's the trade-off.

01:05:25.770 --> 01:05:31.140
If you look at the
different types of formats,

01:05:31.140 --> 01:05:34.540
FP32 used for training
not used for inference.

01:05:34.540 --> 01:05:38.470
Really, BF16 is the
default for inference.

01:05:38.470 --> 01:05:42.570
You can go down to
FP8 or INT8, which

01:05:42.570 --> 01:05:50.100
now is less accurate, but
much cheaper than even FP8.

01:05:50.100 --> 01:05:52.170
So people do do a
bunch of inference

01:05:52.170 --> 01:05:54.550
in INT8, which if you
look at the range, I mean,

01:05:54.550 --> 01:05:58.390
it's an integer between
127 negative 128,

01:05:58.390 --> 01:06:00.210
which is not that--

01:06:00.210 --> 01:06:01.660
it's pretty low precision.

01:06:01.660 --> 01:06:06.660
And people even go down to
INT4, which is they're not.

01:06:06.660 --> 01:06:10.240
INT4 is pretty low.

01:06:10.240 --> 01:06:15.070
There's also other
ways you can do.

01:06:15.070 --> 01:06:22.220
So once you just decide
that you want to quantize,

01:06:22.220 --> 01:06:25.860
I guess you could
do several things.

01:06:25.860 --> 01:06:27.870
You can train with
the quantization,

01:06:27.870 --> 01:06:30.750
but obviously that means you
need to retrain models and more,

01:06:30.750 --> 01:06:33.600
I guess, commonly you do
post-training quantization,

01:06:33.600 --> 01:06:35.420
where you take an
existing model and you

01:06:35.420 --> 01:06:39.620
try to quantize it and try not
to screw things up too much.

01:06:39.620 --> 01:06:44.250
So there's a paper
called LLM-int8(),

01:06:44.250 --> 01:06:47.250
which I'll talk through briefly.

01:06:47.250 --> 01:06:50.750
So in quantization,
basically what

01:06:50.750 --> 01:06:56.810
happens is that you take your
vector, which is let's say FP16,

01:06:56.810 --> 01:06:59.900
and then you need to figure
out the dynamic range.

01:06:59.900 --> 01:07:01.288
If you want to
pack it into INT8,

01:07:01.288 --> 01:07:03.330
you need to figure out
what the largest value is.

01:07:03.330 --> 01:07:08.120
And once you figure that
out, you can divide by that

01:07:08.120 --> 01:07:09.840
and multiply by 128.

01:07:09.840 --> 01:07:11.550
And then you get your integers.

01:07:11.550 --> 01:07:14.900
And then if you
need to dequantize,

01:07:14.900 --> 01:07:17.900
then you go the other way.

01:07:17.900 --> 01:07:20.900
So basically quantization
means that remember memory

01:07:20.900 --> 01:07:21.930
is a bandwidth right.

01:07:21.930 --> 01:07:23.220
So a bottleneck.

01:07:23.220 --> 01:07:28.050
So all your transfers
are happening in date.

01:07:28.050 --> 01:07:33.140
But when you actually
do, I guess you sometimes

01:07:33.140 --> 01:07:35.990
have to upcast to
a floating point

01:07:35.990 --> 01:07:39.440
to actually do the arithmetic.

01:07:39.440 --> 01:07:45.660
So the problem with INT8 is
that not everything fits nicely.

01:07:45.660 --> 01:07:47.810
And you have these
outliers which

01:07:47.810 --> 01:07:50.250
appear in larger networks
that screw things up.

01:07:50.250 --> 01:07:54.660
So what this paper did is
that you take this matrix,

01:07:54.660 --> 01:07:56.910
you identified a really
large outlier values.

01:07:56.910 --> 01:08:00.560
And then you handle them
separately using in full 16-bit

01:08:00.560 --> 01:08:06.830
precision and then do the
most the vast majority INT8.

01:08:06.830 --> 01:08:11.927
So this works well, but
it's actually a bit slower.

01:08:11.927 --> 01:08:14.010
So the motivation here
wasn't the inference speed,

01:08:14.010 --> 01:08:18.390
but more the even being able
to fit your model into memory.

01:08:18.390 --> 01:08:22.080
There's another paper called
Activation-aware quantization.

01:08:22.080 --> 01:08:30.660
And here the idea is that you're
kind of quantizing the weights,

01:08:30.660 --> 01:08:33.590
but you're going to figure
out which weights to quantize

01:08:33.590 --> 01:08:37.550
based on the activations.

01:08:37.550 --> 01:08:42.260
Really quickly, you're
going down to actually INT,

01:08:42.260 --> 01:08:45.649
and this obviously reduces
memory by quite a bit and leads

01:08:45.649 --> 01:08:48.800
to a 3x speed up.

01:08:48.800 --> 01:08:54.450
So the general idea here is
that you get a trained model.

01:08:54.450 --> 01:08:58.250
And it just happens that some
of the weights or activations

01:08:58.250 --> 01:09:00.240
are going to be
abnormally large.

01:09:00.240 --> 01:09:02.450
So for those you
handle separately

01:09:02.450 --> 01:09:08.435
and then everything else you
can work in low precision.

01:09:08.435 --> 01:09:11.850
I'll talk about
model pruning ideas.

01:09:11.850 --> 01:09:13.630
Very light quantization.

01:09:13.630 --> 01:09:15.727
The basic idea is very simple.

01:09:15.727 --> 01:09:17.560
You just rip out parts
of an expensive model

01:09:17.560 --> 01:09:20.200
to make it cheaper,
and then you fix it up.

01:09:20.200 --> 01:09:24.970
So in this NVIDIA paper,
what they do is they first

01:09:24.970 --> 01:09:30.609
identify important either layers
or heads or hidden dimensions

01:09:30.609 --> 01:09:32.660
using a small calibration size.

01:09:32.660 --> 01:09:37.040
They use some simple
scores to compute that.

01:09:37.040 --> 01:09:40.420
And then you just remove
the unimportant layers

01:09:40.420 --> 01:09:42.620
or hidden units or heads.

01:09:42.620 --> 01:09:45.200
And then now if you
just take that model,

01:09:45.200 --> 01:09:48.399
it's going to be clearly worse.

01:09:48.399 --> 01:09:51.550
But so then the last step is
you distill the original model

01:09:51.550 --> 01:09:52.819
into the pruned model.

01:09:52.819 --> 01:09:56.757
So you repair the model
from the initialization,

01:09:56.757 --> 01:09:57.590
which is your prune.

01:09:57.590 --> 01:09:59.120
So you're not
starting from scratch.

01:09:59.120 --> 01:10:02.440
You're starting from something
that's worse but hopefully not

01:10:02.440 --> 01:10:04.480
worse, and hopefully
retains a lot

01:10:04.480 --> 01:10:06.580
of the same
structural properties

01:10:06.580 --> 01:10:08.000
of the original model.

01:10:08.000 --> 01:10:12.150
It's just maybe not kind of
calibrated in some sense.

01:10:12.150 --> 01:10:14.560
And the results are
pretty good on that.

01:10:14.560 --> 01:10:17.700
So they have these 15
billion parameter models that

01:10:17.700 --> 01:10:22.230
they're able to reduce to 8B
with hardly any drop in this,

01:10:22.230 --> 01:10:24.790
I guess, at least
according to MLU,

01:10:24.790 --> 01:10:28.360
and then down to
4B with some drop,

01:10:28.360 --> 01:10:33.720
but you're also going down
quite a bit to 4B model.

01:10:33.720 --> 01:10:38.820
OK, so maybe to summarize
this taking shortcuts idea.

01:10:38.820 --> 01:10:42.168
You can reduce
inference complexity

01:10:42.168 --> 01:10:43.210
without hurting accuracy.

01:10:43.210 --> 01:10:44.918
You can do it from
scratch where you just

01:10:44.918 --> 01:10:47.850
define a fresh architecture
that's by construction fast

01:10:47.850 --> 01:10:49.150
and just train it.

01:10:49.150 --> 01:10:52.390
Or you can distill you
define that architecture.

01:10:52.390 --> 01:10:58.200
You can take a slow model and
you figure out some of scheme

01:10:58.200 --> 01:11:02.320
to initialize the new
model with the old model.

01:11:02.320 --> 01:11:04.886
And then you basically
do distillation.

01:11:10.500 --> 01:11:14.960
So now all of these are
a little bit unsatisfying

01:11:14.960 --> 01:11:17.000
because they're lossy.

01:11:17.000 --> 01:11:20.060
So you get massive speed
ups, but you always

01:11:20.060 --> 01:11:23.960
wonder, well, maybe this model
isn't as actually as good

01:11:23.960 --> 01:11:24.870
as original.

01:11:24.870 --> 01:11:27.500
So speculative decoding
or speculative sampling

01:11:27.500 --> 01:11:31.700
allows you to basically have
your cake and eat it, too.

01:11:31.700 --> 01:11:34.380
So recall there's two
stages of inference.

01:11:34.380 --> 01:11:38.160
You pre-fill which
you're given a sequence,

01:11:38.160 --> 01:11:39.990
you encode all the
tokens in parallel.

01:11:39.990 --> 01:11:42.660
There's a compute-limited,
which is great.

01:11:42.660 --> 01:11:45.590
Notice that this also
gives you log probabilities

01:11:45.590 --> 01:11:47.150
for each of the tokens.

01:11:47.150 --> 01:11:50.257
And then there's generation,
which is one token at a time.

01:11:50.257 --> 01:11:51.090
It's memory-limited.

01:11:51.090 --> 01:11:52.040
It's slow.

01:11:52.040 --> 01:11:56.730
So in other words, checking
is faster than generation.

01:11:56.730 --> 01:11:58.110
So intuitively this makes sense.

01:11:58.110 --> 01:12:00.440
But hopefully now you
also appreciate the math

01:12:00.440 --> 01:12:03.500
behind why this is true.

01:12:03.500 --> 01:12:07.260
And the speculative sampling
idea is actually really,

01:12:07.260 --> 01:12:07.900
really elegant.

01:12:07.900 --> 01:12:12.030
It was proposed in parallel
by these two independent teams

01:12:12.030 --> 01:12:13.860
from Google.

01:12:13.860 --> 01:12:20.040
And the idea is to use
a cheap draft model

01:12:20.040 --> 01:12:24.120
p to just run ahead and
generate some tokens.

01:12:24.120 --> 01:12:26.730
And then you're going
to evaluate those tokens

01:12:26.730 --> 01:12:28.360
with a target model.

01:12:28.360 --> 01:12:31.990
And because evaluation of
given tokens is just prefilled,

01:12:31.990 --> 01:12:34.030
so you can do that in
parallel, which is fast.

01:12:34.030 --> 01:12:35.080
And then you accept it.

01:12:35.080 --> 01:12:36.900
If it looks good.

01:12:36.900 --> 01:12:41.290
So this is what it
looks like in real life.

01:12:41.290 --> 01:12:44.830
So if you're using a big model
generating one token at a time,

01:12:44.830 --> 01:12:45.550
that's slow.

01:12:45.550 --> 01:12:49.290
But in speculative decoding,
you have the draft model

01:12:49.290 --> 01:12:52.770
that's racing ahead,
generating a lot of tokens

01:12:52.770 --> 01:12:56.770
and using the big model
to essentially verify.

01:12:56.770 --> 01:13:00.040
And sometimes it will reject
and sometimes it will accept.

01:13:00.040 --> 01:13:02.010
And the acceptance
rate basically

01:13:02.010 --> 01:13:06.700
determines how fast of
a speed up you have.

01:13:06.700 --> 01:13:11.410
So here is the more
formal algorithm.

01:13:11.410 --> 01:13:14.810
So you're going to
have a look ahead of K.

01:13:14.810 --> 01:13:16.750
So you're going to
use your draft model

01:13:16.750 --> 01:13:19.600
and generate K tokens
autoregressively.

01:13:19.600 --> 01:13:22.030
So this is hopefully fast
because your draft model

01:13:22.030 --> 01:13:22.850
is small.

01:13:22.850 --> 01:13:26.120
And then you're given these
K tokens that you generated.

01:13:26.120 --> 01:13:28.030
And I'm going to
score them based

01:13:28.030 --> 01:13:30.550
on I'm going to compute the
probability under the target

01:13:30.550 --> 01:13:33.820
model Q. Now I'm going
to decide whether I want

01:13:33.820 --> 01:13:35.540
to accept this or not or not.

01:13:35.540 --> 01:13:38.320
So I go through
each token and I'm

01:13:38.320 --> 01:13:43.960
going to essentially accept
it with probability Q over P.

01:13:43.960 --> 01:13:48.760
And the 1 just makes sure
this probabilities are

01:13:48.760 --> 01:13:50.860
between 0 and 1.

01:13:50.860 --> 01:13:53.140
This kind of looks like if
people are familiar with

01:13:53.140 --> 01:13:56.050
Metropolis--Hastings it's kind
of where this kind of comes

01:13:56.050 --> 01:13:57.580
from.

01:13:57.580 --> 01:14:00.947
So intuitively, you're
sampling with P.

01:14:00.947 --> 01:14:03.280
So you need to divide that
out because you don't want P.

01:14:03.280 --> 01:14:07.210
You want Q. So this is
important wait on this.

01:14:07.210 --> 01:14:09.710
So if you accept it, then great.

01:14:09.710 --> 01:14:13.360
You move on and you look at
the next draft token and so on.

01:14:13.360 --> 01:14:16.420
And if you don't
accept it, then you're

01:14:16.420 --> 01:14:22.580
going to sample from the
target model, the slow model,

01:14:22.580 --> 01:14:25.480
but you do this correction
where you've already

01:14:25.480 --> 01:14:26.660
tried to sample using p.

01:14:26.660 --> 01:14:28.202
So you don't need
to do that anymore.

01:14:28.202 --> 01:14:31.300
You subtract it out
and you sample from Q.

01:14:31.300 --> 01:14:34.420
So this is basically kind
of a rejection sampling

01:14:34.420 --> 01:14:40.210
with a proposal P and a target
Q. The only difference is

01:14:40.210 --> 01:14:45.490
that you are sampling
your interaction sampling

01:14:45.490 --> 01:14:48.490
if you reject and you reject
and you just try again

01:14:48.490 --> 01:14:49.870
and you try again.

01:14:49.870 --> 01:14:53.680
And here, we don't want
to keep on looping forever

01:14:53.680 --> 01:14:56.120
because if you reject we're
just going to say, OK, fine,

01:14:56.120 --> 01:14:57.662
we'll bite the bullet
and just sample

01:14:57.662 --> 01:15:01.900
from the more expensive model.

01:15:01.900 --> 01:15:04.560
So the cool thing
here is that you're

01:15:04.560 --> 01:15:09.460
guaranteed to get an exact
sample from the target model.

01:15:09.460 --> 01:15:11.110
So those of you
familiar with sampling,

01:15:11.110 --> 01:15:13.150
this is what shouldn't
be too surprising.

01:15:13.150 --> 01:15:15.660
You're able to use
prior information

01:15:15.660 --> 01:15:18.060
to speed up sampling.

01:15:18.060 --> 01:15:21.750
But in The language modeling
context, this is kind of nice.

01:15:21.750 --> 01:15:23.205
I'm going to skip this.

01:15:23.205 --> 01:15:24.610
This is not really a proof.

01:15:24.610 --> 01:15:28.320
This is just some derivation to
show that for a case of vocab

01:15:28.320 --> 01:15:31.950
too, why this these
formulas give you

01:15:31.950 --> 01:15:36.510
the right unbiased
sampling procedure.

01:15:36.510 --> 01:15:39.640
And it works pretty well.

01:15:39.640 --> 01:15:45.090
So the accuracy should
be actually the same

01:15:45.090 --> 01:15:46.660
since it's the same model.

01:15:46.660 --> 01:15:48.370
But maybe there's
some randomness there.

01:15:48.370 --> 01:15:51.000
But the speedup is you're
getting a factor of 2 speed

01:15:51.000 --> 01:15:52.180
up, essentially.

01:15:56.210 --> 01:16:00.330
So in practice what you do is
you take you have something like

01:16:00.330 --> 01:16:04.610
a 70B model your draft
model is much, much smaller.

01:16:04.610 --> 01:16:08.450
And if your target model is 8B,
then your draft model might be

01:16:08.450 --> 01:16:09.180
1B.

01:16:09.180 --> 01:16:12.050
And you generally want to make
the draft model as closest

01:16:12.050 --> 01:16:14.630
to the target as possible.

01:16:14.630 --> 01:16:17.540
And so if you're doing
some distillation

01:16:17.540 --> 01:16:20.340
that could make it even better.

01:16:20.340 --> 01:16:25.490
There's a bunch of-- this is
a pretty hot area of research

01:16:25.490 --> 01:16:26.400
and inference.

01:16:26.400 --> 01:16:29.720
There's a lot of ways
to improve this process.

01:16:29.720 --> 01:16:33.690
You can use Medusa,
which is this way,

01:16:33.690 --> 01:16:35.570
to have the draft model
instead of generating

01:16:35.570 --> 01:16:38.070
autoregressively sample
multiple tokens in parallel,

01:16:38.070 --> 01:16:41.480
or EAGLE, where you're
actually taking high level

01:16:41.480 --> 01:16:44.240
features of the target model
and pumping them into the draft

01:16:44.240 --> 01:16:45.090
model to generate.

01:16:45.090 --> 01:16:47.790
So the draft model doesn't
actually have to stand alone.

01:16:47.790 --> 01:16:49.850
It can be kind of glommed
on to the target model

01:16:49.850 --> 01:16:52.910
to help it generate.

01:16:52.910 --> 01:16:59.030
So summary exact sampling from
the target model thanks to math.

01:16:59.030 --> 01:17:04.140
And this exploits the symmetry
between checking and generation.

01:17:04.140 --> 01:17:08.580
Or prefill and generation.

01:17:08.580 --> 01:17:11.510
And there's actually a lot of
room for innovation on the draft

01:17:11.510 --> 01:17:17.210
model, which can everything that
we've talked about before, where

01:17:17.210 --> 01:17:19.530
you can have different
radical architectures,

01:17:19.530 --> 01:17:23.130
different ways of quantizing,
all of those apply.

01:17:23.130 --> 01:17:27.470
The only thing is that you
get to basically guarantee

01:17:27.470 --> 01:17:30.350
that you're getting
exact sample.

01:17:30.350 --> 01:17:32.750
So now I'll go--

01:17:32.750 --> 01:17:37.070
I'm out of time, but quickly
go through the question that

01:17:37.070 --> 01:17:41.280
came up earlier, which is that
in practice when you're serving,

01:17:41.280 --> 01:17:42.350
there's live traffic.

01:17:42.350 --> 01:17:44.930
Requests come at
different times.

01:17:44.930 --> 01:17:47.180
They finish at different times.

01:17:47.180 --> 01:17:49.080
Some of them have
shared prefixes.

01:17:49.080 --> 01:17:50.010
Some of them don't.

01:17:50.010 --> 01:17:51.240
They have different lengths.

01:17:51.240 --> 01:17:56.780
So it's very heterogeneous
in comparison to training

01:17:56.780 --> 01:17:59.120
where you get basically
a dense block of tokens,

01:17:59.120 --> 01:18:02.320
and you're basically going
to push it through your GPU

01:18:02.320 --> 01:18:03.860
at full speed.

01:18:03.860 --> 01:18:06.290
So what do you do in this case?

01:18:06.290 --> 01:18:10.630
So there's a series of
papers that explore this.

01:18:10.630 --> 01:18:15.460
And the basic idea is if this
is so the last part is more

01:18:15.460 --> 01:18:18.860
of systems-level contribution.

01:18:18.860 --> 01:18:23.440
So the idea is that you don't
wait for batches to-- the train

01:18:23.440 --> 01:18:23.990
leaves.

01:18:23.990 --> 01:18:27.550
There's no the train
doesn't wait for you.

01:18:27.550 --> 01:18:30.800
So when a new batch comes
you're just going to put it in.

01:18:30.800 --> 01:18:39.280
Which means that the worker
that's generating tokens

01:18:39.280 --> 01:18:42.950
needs to hand control back
to the scheduler every step.

01:18:42.950 --> 01:18:45.130
So you generate a token,
come back to the scheduler

01:18:45.130 --> 01:18:51.440
and say if there's new requests
then, they get stuck in.

01:18:51.440 --> 01:18:53.180
And then it kind of continues.

01:18:53.180 --> 01:18:56.340
So you're kind of not wasting
any s time waiting around

01:18:56.340 --> 01:18:57.960
for requests.

01:18:57.960 --> 01:19:00.340
Now, there's a kind of a
problem with batching, I think,

01:19:00.340 --> 01:19:02.538
which is behind the question.

01:19:02.538 --> 01:19:05.080
Batching works when everything
is at the same dimensionality,

01:19:05.080 --> 01:19:07.270
but every quest might
be a different length.

01:19:07.270 --> 01:19:09.750
So there's this idea
of selective batching

01:19:09.750 --> 01:19:14.560
where you basically break up
your computation for attention.

01:19:14.560 --> 01:19:16.630
Everything has to be
handled separately.

01:19:16.630 --> 01:19:20.530
But for MLPs, remember, which
are the bulk of the computation,

01:19:20.530 --> 01:19:23.760
you can actually take
tensors of different sizes

01:19:23.760 --> 01:19:24.970
and you just flatten them.

01:19:24.970 --> 01:19:30.690
And because they don't interact,
they can just be kind of along

01:19:30.690 --> 01:19:32.350
for the ride in the
batch dimension.

01:19:32.350 --> 01:19:35.370
OK, I know that was fast,
but I'll just quickly

01:19:35.370 --> 01:19:37.620
go over a page attention now.

01:19:37.620 --> 01:19:40.800
This is the paper behind
vLLM, which some of you

01:19:40.800 --> 01:19:42.780
probably have used.

01:19:42.780 --> 01:19:45.460
And this addresses the
memory usage problem.

01:19:45.460 --> 01:19:47.640
So if you have a KV
cache and prompts

01:19:47.640 --> 01:19:50.010
are coming in and
finishing, then your cache

01:19:50.010 --> 01:19:51.280
is going to get fragmented.

01:19:51.280 --> 01:19:53.370
So you're going to have--

01:19:53.370 --> 01:19:57.180
you're going to allocate a
bunch of space for a request,

01:19:57.180 --> 01:19:59.680
but you don't know how many
tokens are going to generate it.

01:19:59.680 --> 01:20:02.427
So there's going to be
internal fragmentation.

01:20:02.427 --> 01:20:05.010
And then there's also going to
be external fragmentation where

01:20:05.010 --> 01:20:08.500
there's padding between
the request and responses.

01:20:08.500 --> 01:20:09.670
So that's no good.

01:20:09.670 --> 01:20:13.260
So the PagedAttention basically
says remember operating systems?

01:20:13.260 --> 01:20:16.750
We have and how
virtual memory works.

01:20:16.750 --> 01:20:20.410
We divide the KV cache into a
sequence of contiguous blocks.

01:20:20.410 --> 01:20:24.640
And then we just put them
wherever we find white space.

01:20:24.640 --> 01:20:27.720
So if you have two
requests coming in,

01:20:27.720 --> 01:20:32.200
then they might just the first
request might be here, here,

01:20:32.200 --> 01:20:34.600
and here and the second
request might be here and here.

01:20:34.600 --> 01:20:36.120
So the blocks are the
things that you're

01:20:36.120 --> 01:20:37.180
going to keep contiguous.

01:20:37.180 --> 01:20:40.410
And that's going to
give allow you time

01:20:40.410 --> 01:20:44.040
to coalesce your memory.

01:20:44.040 --> 01:20:47.160
So you can also
play these tricks

01:20:47.160 --> 01:20:50.580
where if you have a
sharing of prefixes

01:20:50.580 --> 01:20:55.250
then there's another idea from
our operating systems, which

01:20:55.250 --> 01:20:56.430
is copy on write.

01:20:56.430 --> 01:20:59.150
So you basically maintain
reference counters

01:20:59.150 --> 01:21:04.290
for how many basically sequences
are using this particular block.

01:21:04.290 --> 01:21:07.640
And then if you need to
diverge and have blocks

01:21:07.640 --> 01:21:10.190
go in different
directions, then you copy

01:21:10.190 --> 01:21:12.380
and you reduce the
reference count.

01:21:12.380 --> 01:21:14.930
There's a bunch of other
VLM optimizations, which

01:21:14.930 --> 01:21:18.170
I won't go through, but
basically the summary

01:21:18.170 --> 01:21:22.290
is remember your
operating systems classes.

01:21:22.290 --> 01:21:24.740
You can apply them
to inference as well.

01:21:24.740 --> 01:21:26.970
So quick summary.

01:21:26.970 --> 01:21:30.440
Inference is really,
really important.

01:21:30.440 --> 01:21:34.370
and the characteristics
are distinct from training.

01:21:34.370 --> 01:21:36.710
You're memory-limited
and it's also

01:21:36.710 --> 01:21:41.250
dynamic, which leads to a
bunch of new challenges.

01:21:41.250 --> 01:21:43.610
We saw a whole host of
different techniques

01:21:43.610 --> 01:21:46.020
around new architectures
quantization, pruning,

01:21:46.020 --> 01:21:49.760
distillation,
speculative decoding.

01:21:49.760 --> 01:21:54.800
There's ideas from systems which
can allow you to better use

01:21:54.800 --> 01:21:58.310
your memory overlap,
communication, and compute

01:21:58.310 --> 01:22:00.120
and things like that.

01:22:00.120 --> 01:22:04.280
But I would say that there's
probably even more opportunity

01:22:04.280 --> 01:22:06.960
in the modeling
and architecture,

01:22:06.960 --> 01:22:09.500
because if you
think about it all,

01:22:09.500 --> 01:22:13.440
inference narrowly is inference
in a particular model.

01:22:13.440 --> 01:22:15.000
How do I run this
particular model?

01:22:15.000 --> 01:22:17.100
But who cares about
that particular model?

01:22:17.100 --> 01:22:20.540
You care about delivering good
accuracy given your resource

01:22:20.540 --> 01:22:21.180
budget.

01:22:21.180 --> 01:22:28.380
So a lot of these ideas that are
trying to reduce the KV cache,

01:22:28.380 --> 01:22:30.590
changing the transformer
are basically

01:22:30.590 --> 01:22:33.885
ways to sidestep
the problem and say,

01:22:33.885 --> 01:22:35.760
well, I have something
that's more efficient.

01:22:35.760 --> 01:22:39.080
And I can train it in a way
that gets me better accuracy

01:22:39.080 --> 01:22:40.940
then I win.

01:22:40.940 --> 01:22:44.400
OK, so that's all I have and
I will see you next time.

01:22:44.400 --> 01:22:46.810
Then we're back to scaling laws.
